(typeof module !== 'undefined' && module.exports ? data => { module.exports = data; } : data => registerLessonPack('en', data))({
  "crypto-01-01": {"html":"\n<h2>Monoalphabetic Ciphers & English Frequency Analysis</h2>\n\n<p>Classical cryptography constitutes the historical foundation of all modern information security. Across millennia of human communication, <strong>Monoalphabetic Substitution Ciphers</strong>—exemplified by the Roman military's <strong>Caesar Cipher</strong> and the ancient Hebrew <strong>Atbash Cipher</strong>—served as primary security instruments. Although monoalphabetic substitution obfuscates plaintext tokens, 9th-century Arab scholars formalized the definitive method for dismantling them: <strong>Frequency Analysis</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: A Caesar cipher simply disguises the 26 letters of the English alphabet behind masks. If the shift parameter is set to 3, letter A maps to D, and B maps to E. Sherlock Holmes utilized frequency analysis to solve <em>The Adventure of the Dancing Men</em>: In standard English prose, the letter <strong>E</strong> appears with overwhelming statistical regularity (approx. 12.7%), followed by <strong>T</strong> (9.1%) and <strong>A</strong> (8.2%). Regardless of the arbitrary glyph chosen to replace E, simple tallying of character frequencies exposes the substitution immediately!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master monoalphabetic substitution algebra (modulo 26 arithmetic: \\(C \\equiv P + k \\pmod{26}\\))<br>\n  ② Analyze classical variations (ROT13, Affine Ciphers, Atbash mirror transformations)<br>\n  ③ Apply the standardized English letter frequency matrix (ETAOIN SHRDLU) to cryptanalysis<br>\n  ④ Implement Python scripts to automate letter frequency scoring and brute-force recoveries</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Standard English Letter Frequencies Reference (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Character</th><th>Natural Frequency</th><th>Character</th><th>Natural Frequency</th><th>Character</th><th>Natural Frequency</th><th>Common N-grams</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>E</code></td>\n      <td><strong>12.70%</strong> (Dominant)</td>\n      <td><code>O</code></td>\n      <td>7.51%</td>\n      <td><code>M</code></td>\n      <td>2.41%</td>\n      <td><code>TH</code> (High-frequency bigram)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>T</code></td>\n      <td><strong>9.06%</strong></td>\n      <td><code>I</code></td>\n      <td>6.97%</td>\n      <td><code>W</code></td>\n      <td>2.36%</td>\n      <td><code>HE</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>A</code></td>\n      <td><strong>8.17%</strong></td>\n      <td><code>N</code></td>\n      <td>6.75%</td>\n      <td><code>F</code></td>\n      <td>2.23%</td>\n      <td><code>IN</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>S</code></td>\n      <td>6.33%</td>\n      <td><code>R</code></td>\n      <td>5.99%</td>\n      <td><code>G</code></td>\n      <td>2.02%</td>\n      <td><code>ER</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>H</code></td>\n      <td>6.09%</td>\n      <td><code>D</code></td>\n      <td>4.25%</td>\n      <td><code>J, Q, X, Z</code></td>\n      <td><strong>&lt; 0.2%</strong> (Ultra-rare)</td>\n      <td><code>THE</code>, <code>AND</code>, <code>ING</code></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Automated Frequency Analysis in Python</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># caesar_solver.py - Exhaustive shift analysis with frequency heuristic scoring\nimport string\n\ndef crack_caesar(ciphertext: str):\n    \"\"\"Evaluates all 25 potential shifts, ranking outputs by English letter frequencies\"\"\"\n    common_letters = set(\"etaoinshrdlu\")\n    results = []\n    \n    for shift in range(1, 26):\n        decrypted = []\n        for char in ciphertext:\n            if char.isalpha():\n                base = ord('A') if char.isupper() else ord('a')\n                decrypted.append(chr((ord(char) - base - shift) % 26 + base))\n            else:\n                decrypted.append(char)\n        text = \"\".join(decrypted)\n        score = sum(1 for c in text.lower() if c in common_letters)\n        results.append((score, shift, text))\n    \n    results.sort(key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\n    return results[0] # Returns candidate with maximum frequency overlap\n\ncipher = \"KHOOR ZRUOG! WKLV LV D VHFUHW PHVVDJH.\"\nscore, shift, plain = crack_caesar(cipher)\nprint(f\"Optimal shift candidate: {shift}, Decrypted: {plain}\")\n# Output: Optimal shift candidate: 3, Decrypted: HELLO WORLD! THIS IS A SECRET MESSAGE.</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise:</strong><br>\n  What is the shift offset utilized by the ubiquitous Unix and forum obfuscation scheme <code>ROT13</code>, and why does executing ROT13 twice reconstruct the original plaintext?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> The shift offset is 13. Because the Latin alphabet comprises exactly 26 letters and (13 + 13 = 26 equiv 0 pmod{26}), ROT13 is its own mathematical inverse (an involution). The encryption and decryption algorithms are identical.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Monoalphabetic substitution fails because it preserves the underlying unigram statistical frequency distributions of natural language.<br>\n  ② Given sufficient ciphertext volume, frequency analysis combined with bigram and trigram heuristics renders simple substitution trivial to reverse.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"When cryptanalyzing an English monoalphabetic substitution cipher using Frequency Analysis, which letter naturally exhibits the highest frequency?","options":["Letter A","Letter E","Letter T","Letter S"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In standard modern English prose, the letter E exhibits the highest frequency (approx. 12.7%), followed by T (9.1%) and A (8.2%)."},{"question":"The Caesar Cipher is modeled as modulo 26 arithmetic: C = (P + k) mod 26. What is the total size of its key space?","options":["256 possibilities","26 possibilities (25 effective shift keys)","2^56 keys","Infinite"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With 26 alphabet characters, parameter k yields exactly 26 transformations (k=0 being identity). An adversary tests at most 25 candidate shifts to break it completely."}]},
  "crypto-01-02": {"html":"\n<h2>Polyalphabetic Ciphers & The Kasiski Examination</h2>\n\n<p>To eliminate the single-letter statistical vulnerabilities of monoalphabetic substitution, 16th-century French diplomat Blaise de Vigenère refined the <strong>Vigenère Cipher</strong>. Celebrated for over two centuries across European courts as \"the unbreakable cipher\" (<em>Le Chiffre Indéchiffrable</em>), its apparent invulnerability was shattered in 1863 when Prussian officer Friedrich Kasiski published the <strong>Kasiski Examination</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: Monoalphabetic substitution uses a single rigid ruler; the Vigenère cipher <strong>swaps the ruler with every character typed</strong>! Suppose the secret key word is <code>KEY</code> (length 3): character 1 shifts according to K, character 2 according to E, character 3 according to Y, and character 4 wraps around back to K. Even if the word <code>THE</code> appears multiple times in the plaintext, differing alignment against the key shifts it into distinct ciphertext tokens. However, whenever identical words appear at intervals that are multiples of the key length, they produce <strong>identical ciphertext sequences</strong>. Calculating the greatest common divisor across these repeated sequence intervals unmasks the exact key length!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand polyalphabetic substitution mechanics using the Vigenère Tableau algebraic framework<br>\n  ② Master the Kasiski Examination to identify repeated n-grams and determine key lengths via GCD<br>\n  ③ Calculate the Index of Coincidence (IC) to distinguish polyalphabetic from monoalphabetic distributions<br>\n  ④ Decompose polyalphabetic ciphers into independent monoalphabetic streams for automated resolution</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Kasiski Metrics & Index of Coincidence Reference (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Analytical Metric</th><th>Mathematical Definition</th><th>Baseline Standard Values</th><th>Cryptanalytic Application</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Vigenère Encryption</strong></td>\n      <td>(C_i = (P_i + K_{i \bmod L}) \bmod 26)</td>\n      <td>(L) denotes key string length</td>\n      <td>Decryption maps as (P_i = (C_i - K_{i \bmod L} + 26) \bmod 26)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Index of Coincidence (IC)</strong></td>\n      <td>(IC = \frac{sum f_i(f_i - 1)}{N(N - 1)})</td>\n      <td>Natural English: <strong>~0.067</strong><br>Uniform Randomness: <strong>~0.038</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>Differentiates cipher complexity</strong>: An IC of ~0.038 confirms polyalphabetic masking; slicing by key length (L) restores sub-stream ICs to ~0.066</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Kasiski Examination</strong></td>\n      <td>Isolates repeated sequences of length (ge 3); measures distances (d_1, d_2, ...)</td>\n      <td>Common divisor intersections across observed spans</td>\n      <td><strong>Isolates Key Length</strong>: Key length (L) is overwhelmingly likely to divide the greatest common divisor of observed intervals</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Practical Kasiski Interval Decomposition in Python</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># kasiski_analyzer.py - Identifies repeated substrings and calculates divisor intervals\nfrom math import gcd\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nfrom functools import reduce\n\ndef find_repeated_substrings(ciphertext: str, seq_len: int = 3):\n    clean_text = \"\".join(filter(str.isalpha, ciphertext.upper()))\n    positions = defaultdict(list)\n    \n    for i in range(len(clean_text) - seq_len + 1):\n        seq = clean_text[i:i + seq_len]\n        positions[seq].append(i)\n    \n    distances = []\n    for seq, pos_list in positions.items():\n        if len(pos_list) > 1:\n            for i in range(len(pos_list) - 1):\n                distances.append(pos_list[i+1] - pos_list[i])\n            print(f\"Repeated n-gram '{seq}' at positions {pos_list}, delta: {[pos_list[k+1]-pos_list[k] for k in range(len(pos_list)-1)]}\")\n    \n    return distances\n\nmock_distances = [24, 36, 60]\nguessed_key_len = reduce(gcd, mock_distances)\nprint(f\"GCD-Derived Key Length: {guessed_key_len}\")\n# Output: GCD-Derived Key Length: 12</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise:</strong><br>\n  An encrypted message yields an overall Index of Coincidence of <code>0.039</code>. When partitioned into 5 interleaved sub-streams, the IC of each individual stream surges to <code>0.066</code>. What does this confirm?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> This mathematically proves that the secret key length is exactly 5. The aggregate score of 0.039 reflects the entropy of polyalphabetic flattening; slicing into 5 streams partitions the message such that every 5th letter was encrypted using an identical shift key. Each sub-stream is reduced to a standard Caesar cipher and can be solved with frequency analysis.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Polyalphabetic substitution relies on key cyclicity; repeating keys creates predictable resonance intervals.<br>\n  ② The combination of Kasiski interval mapping and Index of Coincidence computation neutralizes classic polyalphabetic algorithms.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"To which category of classical ciphers does the Vigenère Cipher belong?","options":["Monoalphabetic substitution","Polyalphabetic substitution","Transposition cipher","Stream cipher"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Vigenère cipher uses a periodic keyword where different positions shift according to alternating rows of the tabula recta, constituting a polyalphabetic substitution."},{"question":"Which statistical methodology is primarily leveraged by cryptanalysts to deduce the keyword length of a Vigenère cipher?","options":["Avalanche coefficient analysis","Index of Coincidence (IC) and Kasiski Examination","Hamming distance calculation","Modular inverse derivation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Kasiski examination identifies GCDs among repeated n-gram distances, while the Index of Coincidence (IC) matches observed unigram distributions against natural language baselines."}]},
  "crypto-01-03": {"html":"\n<h2>One-Time Pad (OTP) & Perfect Secrecy</h2>\n\n<p>Among all cryptographic mechanisms discovered throughout human history, exactly one construction has been mathematically proven <strong>impervious to cryptanalysis regardless of adversarial computational power</strong>: the <strong>One-Time Pad (OTP)</strong>, invented by Gilbert Vernam in 1917 and rigorously proven by Claude Shannon in his landmark 1949 treatise.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: Suppose your message is <code>HELLO</code>. An adversary intercepts the ciphertext and attempts exhaustive key searches across every supercomputer in existence. Because the key was truly random, trial decryptions generate <code>HELLO</code>, <code>APPLE</code>, <code>WATER</code>, <code>MONEY</code>, and <code>KILLU</code> with exactly equal probability! No statistical test can distinguish the genuine message from arbitrary candidates. This is Shannon's <strong>Perfect Secrecy</strong>. However, perfection carries ironclad constraints: the pad must be generated from true physical entropy, match or exceed the message length, and <strong>never be reused under any circumstance</strong>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand Shannon's Perfect Secrecy theorem (\\(P(M=m | C=c) = P(M=m)\\))<br>\n  ② Master the three non-negotiable rules of One-Time Pads (True randomness, equal length, zero reuse)<br>\n  ③ Dissect the mathematical vulnerability of Two-Time Pad key reuse (\\(C_1 \\oplus C_2 = P_1 \\oplus P_2\\))<br>\n  ④ Execute Crib Dragging attacks in Python to recover plaintexts from reused OTP keystreams</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Shannon Perfect Secrecy & Keystream Reuse Reference (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Core Dimension</th><th>Mathematical Formulation</th><th>Operational Security Constraints</th><th>Adversarial Impact of Violations</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Perfect Secrecy</strong></td>\n      <td>(P(M = m mid C = c) = P(M = m))</td>\n      <td>A posteriori probability matches a priori probability; ciphertext yields zero information regarding plaintext</td>\n      <td>If key entropy is less than message entropy ((H(K) &lt; H(M))), perfect secrecy is mathematically impossible</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>OTP Encryption & Decryption</strong></td>\n      <td>(C = P oplus K)<br>(P = C oplus K)</td>\n      <td>Key stream must be sourced from non-deterministic physical entropy (e.g. radioactive decay or thermal noise)</td>\n      <td>Algorithmic PRNG generators degrade OTP into stream ciphers subject to seed prediction</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Two-Time Pad Flaw<br>(Keystream Reuse)</strong></td>\n      <td>(C_1 oplus C_2 = (P_1 oplus K) oplus (P_2 oplus K) = P_1 oplus P_2)</td>\n      <td><strong>Keystreams must never be reused</strong>; pads must be destroyed immediately post-transmission</td>\n      <td>XORing two ciphertexts eliminates the secret key completely, leaving raw plaintext interaction ((P_1 oplus P_2)) solvable via Crib Dragging</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Two-Time Pad Crib Dragging Demonstration</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># two_time_pad_attack.py - Demonstrating keystream cancellation under pad reuse\ndef xor_bytes(b1: bytes, b2: bytes) -> bytes:\n    return bytes(x ^ y for x, y in zip(b1, b2))\n\nkey = b\"SuperSecretPad123\"\nmsg1 = b\"Attack at 0600am\"\nmsg2 = b\"Send more troops\"\n\nc1 = xor_bytes(msg1, key)\nc2 = xor_bytes(msg2, key)\n\n# An eavesdropper intercepts c1 and c2, calculating their mutual XOR:\nc1_xor_c2 = xor_bytes(c1, c2)\nprint(\"c1 ⊕ c2 result:\", c1_xor_c2)\n\n# In ASCII, XORing a letter with a space (0x20) flips its case.\n# By guessing the presence of the word 'Attack' (Crib Dragging):\ncrib = b\"Attack\"\nguess_p2 = xor_bytes(c1_xor_c2[:len(crib)], crib)\nprint(\"Recovered prefix of msg2 using crib 'Attack':\", guess_p2)\n# Output: Recovered prefix of msg2 using crib 'Attack': b'Send m'</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise:</strong><br>\n  If the One-Time Pad is mathematically unbreakable, why is modern digital commerce (HTTPS, banking, cloud computing) secured via AES and RSA instead of OTP?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> Due to the insurmountable <strong>Key Distribution Problem</strong>. OTP mandates that keys must match message volume and never be recycled. Transmitting 1 TB of confidential corporate data would require first securely courier-shipping a 1 TB physical hard drive containing the pad. If an out-of-band channel secure enough to ship 1 TB of keys already existed, one could simply transport the sensitive data across it directly. Modern ciphers trade information-theoretic security for computational security, protecting petabytes with compact 256-bit keys.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① The One-Time Pad guarantees unconditional security if and only if keys are truly random, of equal length, and used once.<br>\n  ② Keystream reuse immediately eliminates key secrecy, reducing the problem to dual-language crib dragging.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Which of the following is NOT an essential prerequisite for achieving Shannon's Perfect Secrecy with a One-Time Pad (OTP)?","options":["The key must be generated by a true hardware random physical source","The key length must be equal to or greater than the plaintext length","The key must never be reused and destroyed immediately after transmission","The key must be distributed using RSA asymmetric encryption"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Shannon's mathematical proof requires true randomness, equality of key length, and absolute single-use. Relying on RSA introduces computational assumptions rather than information-theoretic secrecy."},{"question":"If an adversary intercepts two ciphertexts C1 and C2 encrypted under the exact same OTP key K, what occurs when evaluating C1 ⊕ C2?","options":["The output remains indistinguishable from white noise","The key K is eliminated, exposing the mutual XOR of plaintexts (P1 ⊕ P2)","The original key K is recovered directly","A decryption exception is raised"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Evaluating C1 ⊕ C2 = (P1 ⊕ K) ⊕ (P2 ⊕ K) = P1 ⊕ P2 cancels key K completely, allowing crib-dragging techniques to recover both underlying messages."}]},
  "crypto-02-01": {"html":"\n<h2>Feistel Networks & DES/3DES Architecture Reference</h2>\n\n<p>By the 1970s, rapid advancements in computer processing power rendered classical ciphers obsolete. In 1977, the U.S. National Bureau of Standards (NBS, now NIST) formalized IBM's Lucifer cipher design as the first federal standard for electronic security: the <strong>Data Encryption Standard (DES)</strong>. DES established foundational principles of modern symmetric block cipher engineering, built upon the elegant symmetrical framework invented by Horst Feistel: the <strong>Feistel Network</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: A Feistel network processes data like a baker repeatedly folding dough. A 64-bit block of dough is divided into two halves: Left (L) and Right (R). During each round, only the right half is modified: R passes through an intricate cookie-cutter mold (the Round Function F), and its output stamps color onto the left half (XOR addition). The stamped left half becomes the new right half, while the original, unmodified right half shifts to the left position! The brilliance of this design is mathematical: <strong>encryption and decryption algorithms are identical</strong>—hardware implementations execute inverse operations simply by reversing the schedule of subkeys, slashing silicon manufacturing costs!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand the mathematical invertibility of Feistel networks without requiring invertible round functions<br>\n  ② Master DES baseline parameters (64-bit blocks, 56-bit effective keys, 16-round iteration)<br>\n  ③ Analyze why 2DES collapses under the Meet-in-the-Middle attack, reducing complexity to \\(2^{57}\\)<br>\n  ④ Evaluate Triple-DES (3DES EDE mode) and its vulnerability to 64-bit collision attacks (Sweet32)</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: DES Family Parameters & Cryptanalysis Reference (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Cipher Variant</th><th>Block Size</th><th>Key Length (Effective Strength)</th><th>Structure & Rounds</th><th>Security Posture & Known Vulnerabilities</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Standard DES</strong></td>\n      <td>64 bits (8 bytes)</td>\n      <td>64 bits (<strong>56 bits effective</strong>; 8 bits reserved for parity checks)</td>\n      <td>16-round Feistel network</td>\n      <td><strong>Deprecated / Broken</strong>: 56-bit key search space ((2^{56})) was brute-forced by the EFF Deep Crack machine in under 22 hours in 1999</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Double DES (2DES)<br>(C = E_{K2}(E_{K1}(P)))</strong></td>\n      <td>64 bits</td>\n      <td>128 bits (Expected 112 bits of security)</td>\n      <td>32-round Feistel network</td>\n      <td><strong>Architectural Failure</strong>: Vulnerable to <strong>Meet-in-the-Middle</strong> attacks; space-time trade-offs reduce effective complexity to (2^{57}), making it barely stronger than single DES</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Triple DES (3DES)<br>EDE Mode</strong></td>\n      <td>64 bits</td>\n      <td>192 bits (<strong>112 or 168 bits effective</strong> across 2 or 3 distinct keys)</td>\n      <td>48-round Feistel (Encrypt-Decrypt-Encrypt)</td>\n      <td><strong>Legacy Transition</strong>: Immune to brute force, but small 64-bit block size enables <strong>Sweet32</strong> birthday attacks (birthday collisions after ~32GB of data)</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Mathematical Proof of Meet-in-the-Middle Attack on 2DES</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># 2des_mitm_concept.py - Meet-in-the-Middle conceptual complexity reduction\n# Given known plaintext-ciphertext pair (P, C): C = E_k2(E_k1(P))\n# Rewritten across the intermediate state: E_k1(P) = D_k2(C)\n\ndef demonstrate_mitm_complexity():\n    # Naive Exhaustive Brute-Force:\n    # Test all pairs of (k1, k2) -> 2^56 * 2^56 = 2^112 operations (computationally impossible)\n    \n    # Meet-in-the-Middle (Space-Time Trade-off):\n    # Phase 1: Compute Mid = E_k1(P) for all 2^56 values of k1; store in lookup table {Mid: k1}\n    # Phase 2: Compute Mid' = D_k2(C) for all 2^56 values of k2; query hash table for collision\n    # Total computation: 2^56 encryptions + 2^56 decryptions = 2 * 2^56 = 2^57\n    \n    print(\"Standard 2DES Brute Force Complexity: 2^112\")\n    print(\"Meet-in-the-Middle Attack Complexity: 2^57 operations!\")\n    print(\"Result: 112-bit security is crushed into 57-bit practical attack terrain.\")\n\ndemonstrate_mitm_complexity()</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise:</strong><br>\n  In Triple-DES (3DES), why was the operation standard configured as Encrypt-Decrypt-Encrypt (EDE) rather than intuitive Encrypt-Encrypt-Encrypt (EEE)?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> To ensure <strong>backward compatibility</strong> with legacy single-DES hardware. If a system operator configures 3DES with three identical keys ((K_1 = K_2 = K_3)), the second stage Decrypt operation precisely cancels the first stage Encrypt operation ((D_K(E_K(P)) = P)), leaving the final Encrypt stage to produce an output identical to standard single DES.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Feistel architectures decouple encryption invertibility from round function reversibility.<br>\n  ② Short 56-bit keys and 64-bit blocks doomed the DES architecture, necessitating the modern AES competition.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the primary engineering advantage of Horst Feistel's symmetric cipher network?","options":["Decryption mirrors the exact sequence of encryption, requiring only the reversal of subkey schedules","It is inherently immune to polynomial-time quantum attacks","The round function F must strictly form a bijective invertible mapping","It eliminates the need for round key scheduling"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Feistel structure guarantees algebraic invertibility regardless of round function complexity: Decryption logic is identical to encryption with reversed subkeys."},{"question":"Why is Double DES (2DES, 112-bit key length) strictly prohibited in security architectures?","options":["Ciphertext output doubles in size","A Meet-in-the-Middle attack collapses its effective security down to 2^57 operations","It cannot execute on 64-bit operating systems","It cannot operate under CBC cipher modes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Meet-in-the-Middle trades memory for time: Encrypting forward from plaintext and decrypting backward from ciphertext hits a midpoint collision in 2^57 complexity."}]},
  "crypto-02-02": {"html":"\n<h2>AES Architecture: S-Box, ShiftRows & MixColumns</h2>\n\n<p>To establish a replacement for the constrained DES architecture, NIST initiated an international competition in 1997. After five years of cryptanalysis, the <strong>Rijndael cipher</strong>, engineered by Belgian cryptographers Joan Daemen and Vincent Rijmen, emerged triumphant. Formalized in 2001 as the <strong>Advanced Encryption Standard (AES, FIPS 197)</strong>, it remains the gold standard for symmetric encryption worldwide.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: AES abandons the Feistel network in favor of a <strong>Substitution-Permutation Network (SPN)</strong>. It arranges each 16-byte message block into a <strong>4×4 State Matrix</strong>. Each encryption round operates like twisting a Rubik's cube: Step 1, every byte is exchanged for a non-linear value via a cryptographic look-up table (<strong>SubBytes</strong>); Step 2, rows 1, 2, and 3 are cyclically shifted left by 1, 2, and 3 positions respectively (<strong>ShiftRows</strong>); Step 3, columns are blended algebraically in a finite field matrix multiplier (<strong>MixColumns</strong>); Step 4, round keys are mixed via bitwise XOR (<strong>AddRoundKey</strong>)—repeating this cycle 10 to 14 times transforms plaintext into mathematical white noise!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Dissect the 4×4 State Matrix and Galois Field \\(\\text{GF}(2^8)\\) arithmetic foundations<br>\n  ② Master the four canonical round transformations: SubBytes, ShiftRows, MixColumns, AddRoundKey<br>\n  ③ Contrast specification parameters across AES-128, AES-192, and AES-256<br>\n  ④ Understand how AES-NI hardware instruction sets achieve multi-gigabyte-per-second encryption throughput</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: AES Round Transformations Reference (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Round Step</th><th>Name</th><th>Mathematical Operation</th><th>Cryptographic Purpose</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1. SubBytes</strong></td>\n      <td>Byte Substitution</td>\n      <td>Non-linear inversion in (\text{GF}(2^8)) combined with affine transformation over the <strong>S-Box</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>Confusion</strong>: Implements the sole non-linear component of AES, neutralizing linear and differential cryptanalysis</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>2. ShiftRows</strong></td>\n      <td>Row Permutation</td>\n      <td>Row 0 unshifted; Row 1 shifted 1 byte left; Row 2 shifted 2 bytes left; Row 3 shifted 3 bytes left</td>\n      <td><strong>Diffusion</strong>: Prevents column isolation, propagating byte modifications into distinct columns for subsequent rounds</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>3. MixColumns</strong></td>\n      <td>Column Mixing</td>\n      <td>Multiplies each column vector by a fixed MDS polynomial matrix over (\text{GF}(2^8)) (Omitted in final round)</td>\n      <td><strong>High-Order Diffusion</strong>: Guarantees that modifying a single input bit diffuses across all 128 bits within two rounds</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>4. AddRoundKey</strong></td>\n      <td>Key Injection</td>\n      <td>Bitwise XOR operation between the 128-bit state matrix and the 128-bit round subkey</td>\n      <td><strong>Security Anchor</strong>: Injects the expanded master secret key material into the ongoing cipher state</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: AES Specifications & OpenSSL CLI Diagnostics</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Cipher Standard</th><th>Key Length (Bits)</th><th>Block Size (Bits)</th><th>Round Count (Nr)</th><th>Expanded Subkey Footprint</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>AES-128</strong></td>\n      <td>128 bits (16 bytes)</td>\n      <td>128 bits (16 bytes)</td>\n      <td><strong>10 rounds</strong></td>\n      <td>11 subkeys (176 bytes)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>AES-192</strong></td>\n      <td>192 bits (24 bytes)</td>\n      <td>128 bits (16 bytes)</td>\n      <td><strong>12 rounds</strong></td>\n      <td>13 subkeys (208 bytes)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>AES-256</strong></td>\n      <td>256 bits (32 bytes)</td>\n      <td>128 bits (16 bytes)</td>\n      <td><strong>14 rounds</strong></td>\n      <td>15 subkeys (240 bytes)</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># 1. Encrypt text using OpenSSL AES-256-CBC with PBKDF2 key derivation\necho \"Classified Intelligence Payload\" | openssl enc -aes-256-cbc -base64 -pass pass:TargetSecretKey2026 -pbkdf2\n\n# 2. Verify AES-NI hardware instruction set extensions on Linux:\ngrep -m1 -o aes /proc/cpuinfo\n# Output 'aes' confirms silicon-level acceleration executing at over 4 GB/sec!</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise:</strong><br>\n  Why is the <code>MixColumns</code> step deliberately omitted during the final round (round 10, 12, or 14) of AES?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> To ensure algorithmic symmetry between encryption and decryption circuits. If the final round included MixColumns, the decryption algorithm would be forced to execute an inverse MixColumns before the first AddRoundKey, complicating circuit layouts. Omission allows decryption to mirror encryption cleanly.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① AES uses a 128-bit state matrix governed by the Substitution-Permutation Network paradigm.<br>\n  ② SubBytes provides non-linear confusion, while ShiftRows and MixColumns deliver rapid multi-round diffusion.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES), which round transformation introduces non-linear confusion?","options":["ShiftRows","SubBytes (S-Box)","MixColumns","AddRoundKey"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SubBytes constitutes the sole non-linear transformation layer within AES, applying multiplicative inversion over GF(2^8) followed by affine mappings to resist differential cryptanalysis."},{"question":"How many transformation rounds are executed by AES-128, AES-192, and AES-256 respectively?","options":["16, 32, and 64 rounds","10, 12, and 14 rounds","8, 10, and 12 rounds","12, 16, and 20 rounds"],"answer":1,"explanation":"FIPS 197 specifies fixed 128-bit block sizes with round schedules calibrated to key size: 10 rounds for 128-bit, 12 rounds for 192-bit, and 14 rounds for 256-bit keys."}]},
  "crypto-02-03": {"html":"\n<h2>Block Cipher Modes: ECB, CBC, GCM & Padding Oracle</h2>\n\n<p>The core AES primitive encrypts only a single, fixed 16-byte block of plaintext. Securing arbitrarily sized files, network packets, or database archives requires selecting a <strong>Mode of Operation</strong> and a <strong>Padding Scheme</strong>. Flawed mode selection causes catastrophic compromises—such as image outline leakage under <strong>Electronic Codebook (ECB)</strong> mode or plaintext extraction via <strong>Padding Oracle</strong> side-channel attacks.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: <strong>ECB mode</strong> is like stamping identical packages with the same seal in isolation. If a digital image features a white background, every 16-byte white block produces the exact same ciphertext byte block! When the famous Linux Penguin logo is encrypted via ECB, its outline remains clearly visible. Conversely, <strong>CBC (Cipher Block Chaining)</strong> operates like dominoes: each plaintext block is XORed with the preceding ciphertext block before encryption, randomized initially by an <strong>Initialization Vector (IV)</strong>. Finally, modern <strong>GCM (Galois/Counter Mode)</strong> incorporates an unforgeable cryptographic authenticity tag (AEAD) to detect tampering instantly!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Evaluate the five classic cipher modes (ECB, CBC, CFB, OFB, GCM) across parallelism and security<br>\n  ② Master PKCS#7 padding validation mechanics (padding with N bytes of value N)<br>\n  ③ Dissect the mathematical mechanics of Padding Oracle attacks to decrypt CBC ciphertexts byte-by-byte<br>\n  ④ Understand why modern production applications mandate Authenticated Encryption (AEAD / AES-GCM)</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Cipher Modes Comparison Reference (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Operating Mode</th><th>Chaining Mechanism</th><th>Initialization Vector (IV) Policy</th><th>Security Posture & Vulnerability Profile</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>ECB<br>(Electronic Codebook)</strong></td>\n      <td>Independent blocks: (C_i = E_K(P_i))</td>\n      <td>No IV</td>\n      <td><strong>Catastrophically Insecure</strong>: Identical plaintext blocks produce identical ciphertext blocks; leaks statistical patterns; vulnerable to block splicing</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>CBC<br>(Cipher Block Chaining)</strong></td>\n      <td>Chained XOR: (C_i = E_K(P_i oplus C_{i-1}))</td>\n      <td><strong>Must be unpredictable and cryptographically random</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>Legacy Standard</strong>: Hides data patterns; sequential-only encryption; vulnerable to <strong>Padding Oracle</strong> attacks when decrypt errors are exposed</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>CTR<br>(Counter Mode)</strong></td>\n      <td>Keystream XOR: (C_i = P_i oplus E_K(Nonce | Counter))</td>\n      <td>Nonce must <strong>never be reused</strong> under the same key</td>\n      <td><strong>High-Speed Stream Mode</strong>: Zero padding required; fully parallelizable; vulnerable to bit-flipping if unauthenticated</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>GCM<br>(Galois/Counter Mode)</strong></td>\n      <td>CTR encryption combined with GMAC Galois polynomial authentication (AEAD)</td>\n      <td><strong>Strictly unique IV</strong> (standard 96-bit length)</td>\n      <td><strong>Modern Industry Standard</strong>: Provides high-throughput parallel performance with cryptographic integrity verification</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Padding Oracle Attack Walkthrough</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># padding_oracle_concept.py - Padding Oracle mathematical derivation\n# In CBC decryption: P_2 = D_K(C_2) ^ C_1\n# PKCS#7 rules: If 1 byte needed, append 0x01; if 2 bytes needed, append 0x02 0x02...\n\ndef explain_padding_oracle_step():\n    print(\"=== Padding Oracle Attack Mechanics ===\")\n    print(\"1. Attacker intercepts cipher blocks C1 and C2, seeking to recover P2[15].\")\n    print(\"2. Attacker crafts a forged C1' block and queries the decryption oracle: Server.decrypt(C1' + C2)\")\n    print(\"3. Attacker iterates C1'[15] from 0x00 to 0xFF:\")\n    print(\"   When the decrypted byte ends with valid PKCS#7 padding (0x01), the server returns HTTP 200 instead of HTTP 500!\")\n    print(\"4. Mathematical invariant at match: D_K(C2)[15] ^ C1'[15] == 0x01\")\n    print(\"   Intermediate state: Intermediate[15] = C1'[15] ^ 0x01\")\n    print(\"5. Plaintext recovery: P2[15] = Intermediate[15] ^ C1[15] (where C1 is the genuine original block)\")\n    print(\"-> Average queries required per byte: 128. Full 16-byte block recovered in ~2048 requests without knowing key K!\")\n\nexplain_padding_oracle_step()</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise:</strong><br>\n  What is the industry-standard remediation for completely eliminating Padding Oracle vulnerabilities from application architectures?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> Transitioning from unauthenticated CBC modes to <strong>Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data (AEAD)</strong> modes such as <strong>AES-GCM</strong> or <strong>ChaCha20-Poly1305</strong>. Under AEAD, the cipher verifies the authentication tag (MAC) prior to attempting payload decryption. If any bit has been tampered with, the request is rejected with a generic authentication error before padding verification can take place.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① ECB leaks macroscopic structural patterns; CBC without authentication is vulnerable to Padding Oracle extraction.<br>\n  ② Modern engineering standards mandate authenticated encryption (AES-GCM) to ensure both confidentiality and message integrity.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Why is Electronic Codebook (ECB) mode deemed insecure for bulk data encryption?","options":["It prevents parallel multicore execution","Identical plaintext blocks produce identical ciphertext blocks, exposing data patterns","Ciphertext size expands by a factor of ten","It mandates dynamic elliptic curve parameter exchanges"],"answer":1,"explanation":"ECB encrypts each block in total isolation without chaining or IVs. Repeating patterns in plaintexts translate directly into repeating patterns in ciphertexts (e.g., the ECB penguin)."},{"question":"What core environmental signal enables a Padding Oracle attack to decrypt CBC ciphertext byte-by-byte?","options":["A physical leak of the server's private key","Differentiated server responses (or timing discrepancies) indicating PKCS#7 padding validation errors","A proven mathematical backdoor inside the AES S-box","An exposed initialization vector IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Padding Oracle attacks exploit distinct server responses regarding whether PKCS#7 padding was valid upon decryption, turning the validation check into a decryption oracle."}]},
  "crypto-03-01": {"html":"\n<h2>Diffie-Hellman Key Exchange & DLP Foundations</h2>\n\n<p>Prior to 1976, all cryptographic communications suffered from an intractable structural vulnerability—<strong>the key distribution dilemma</strong>: To communicate securely, parties had to exchange a shared secret key, but doing so required an already-secure channel. In their seminal 1976 paper <em>New Directions in Cryptography</em>, Whitfield Diffie and Martin Hellman introduced the <strong>Diffie-Hellman Key Exchange (DH)</strong>, enabling two parties to establish a mutual cryptographic secret across a completely untrusted, monitored channel.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: <strong>The Public Color-Mixing Model</strong>.\nImagine Alice and Bob wish to agree upon a secret paint color while an adversary, Eve, observes every transaction:\n① <strong>Public Base Color</strong>: Alice and Bob publicly agree on an initial color (e.g., bright yellow) visible to everyone;\n② <strong>Secret Private Colors</strong>: Alice secretly selects a private red paint; Bob secretly selects a private blue paint. Neither discloses their private color;\n③ <strong>Mixing & Public Exchange</strong>: Alice mixes her private red with public yellow, producing orange, and sends it to Bob. Bob mixes his private blue with public yellow, producing green, and sends it to Alice. Even though Eve inspects the orange and green mixtures, physical pigment chemistry prevents her from separating them back into pure components;\n④ <strong>Terminal Shared Secret</strong>: Alice adds her secret red into Bob's green mixture; Bob adds his secret blue into Alice's orange mixture. Both now possess an identical shade of brown (<strong>Yellow + Red + Blue</strong>)! Eve, possessing only yellow, orange, and green without the secret primaries, can never recreate the shared tone!</p>\n\n<div id=\"diagram-dh\" class=\"interactive-diagram-container\">\n  <!-- Diffie-Hellman dynamic color-mixing & modular arithmetic diagram mounted by frontend -->\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand the mathematical foundations of the Discrete Logarithm Problem (DLP)<br>\n  ② Master the four-step classical Diffie-Hellman protocol: Prime modulus \\(p\\), generator \\(g\\), and key derivation<br>\n  ③ Analyze Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman (ECDH) and Ephemeral Forward Secrecy (ECDHE)<br>\n  ④ Detect classical Man-in-the-Middle (MITM) attacks and implement digital signature authentication</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Diffie-Hellman & ECDH Parameters Reference (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Protocol Variant</th><th>Underlying Math Problem</th><th>Key Size (256-bit Security Level)</th><th>Quantum Resistance</th><th>Primary Production Use Case</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Classical DH (MODP)</strong></td>\n      <td>Discrete Logarithm Problem (DLP) over finite cyclic groups</td>\n      <td>Modulus (p ge 3072 \text{ bits}) (Large keys)</td>\n      <td>Broken (Shor's Algorithm in polynomial time)</td>\n      <td>Legacy IPsec / IKEv1, historic SSH handshakes</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>ECDH (Elliptic Curve)</strong></td>\n      <td>Elliptic Curve Discrete Logarithm Problem (ECDLP: (Q = d cdot G))</td>\n      <td>Curve key <strong>only 256 bits</strong> (Compact & efficient)</td>\n      <td>Broken (Shor's Algorithm in polynomial time)</td>\n      <td>TLS 1.2 / TLS 1.3, Signal Protocol, Apple iMessage</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>ECDHE (Ephemeral)</strong></td>\n      <td>Fresh ephemeral key pairs generated per session exchange</td>\n      <td>Destroyed immediately upon session teardown</td>\n      <td>Provides <strong>Perfect Forward Secrecy (PFS)</strong></td>\n      <td>Modern HTTPS baseline; past traffic safe if server long-term key leaks</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Four-Step Diffie-Hellman Mathematical Sequence</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Phase</th><th>Alice (Initiator)</th><th>Public Channel (Observed by Eve)</th><th>Bob (Responder)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1. Public Parameter Selection</strong></td>\n      <td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align: center;\">Publicly agree upon prime (p) and primitive root generator (g) (e.g., (p = 23, g = 5))</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>2. Keypair Computation</strong></td>\n      <td>Selects private (a = 6)<br>Computes public (A = g^a \bmod p = 5^6 \bmod 23 = 8)</td>\n      <td>Public transmit (A = 8) (\to)<br>(leftarrow) Public transmit (B = 19)</td>\n      <td>Selects private (b = 15)<br>Computes public (B = g^b \bmod p = 5^{15} \bmod 23 = 19)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>3. Shared Secret Computation</strong></td>\n      <td>Receives (B), computes:<br>(K = B^a \bmod p = 19^6 \bmod 23 = mathbf{2})</td>\n      <td>Eve observes (p=23, g=5, A=8, B=19)<br>Cannot compute (a) or (b) in realistic time</td>\n      <td>Receives (A), computes:<br>(K = A^b \bmod p = 8^{15} \bmod 23 = mathbf{2})</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>4. Identity Verification</strong></td>\n      <td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align: center;\">Mathematical identity: (K = (g^b)^a equiv g^{ab} equiv (g^a)^b pmod p). Both derive identical (K = 2)!</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Minimal Diffie-Hellman Protocol in Python</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n  <div class=\"copy-btn\">Copy</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\"># Minimal Diffie-Hellman demonstration script\nimport random\n\n# 1. Public prime p and generator g (Miniature RFC 3526 parameters)\np = 353   # Miniature prime for pedagogical demonstration\ng = 3     # 3 is a primitive root modulo 353\n\n# 2. Alice generates secret key a and computes public value A\nalice_private_a = random.randint(2, p - 2)\nalice_public_A = pow(g, alice_private_a, p)\n\n# 3. Bob generates secret key b and computes public value B\nbob_private_b = random.randint(2, p - 2)\nbob_public_B = pow(g, bob_private_b, p)\n\n# 4. Each party independently derives the shared symmetric key\nalice_shared_key = pow(bob_public_B, alice_private_a, p)\nbob_shared_key = pow(alice_public_A, bob_private_b, p)\n\nprint(f\"Alice computed shared key: {alice_shared_key}\")\nprint(f\"Bob   computed shared key: {bob_shared_key}\")\nassert alice_shared_key == bob_shared_key, \"Keys must match perfectly!\"\nprint(\"✓ DH exchange successful: Shared secret established over open channel!\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercises:</strong><br>\n  Classical Diffie-Hellman withstands passive eavesdropping. Why does it remain utterly vulnerable to active <strong>Man-in-the-Middle (MITM)</strong> attacks, and how do modern cryptographic systems remediate this?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> Native DH lacks an <strong>authentication mechanism</strong>. An active adversary, Mallory, can intercept communications and execute independent DH handshakes with both Alice and Bob, establishing two separate keys and transparently relaying modified traffic. Modern standards mitigate this by combining ephemeral DH with <strong>digital signatures and Public Key Infrastructure (such as TLS certificates signing DH ephemeral public keys)</strong>, ensuring the identity of the remote peer is verifiable.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① DH leverages the one-way nature of the Discrete Logarithm Problem to solve the symmetric key distribution paradox.<br>\n  ② ECDHE incorporates ephemeral elliptic curve parameters to achieve Perfect Forward Secrecy across modern web transport.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"On which number-theoretic hard problem is the Diffie-Hellman Key Exchange based?","options":["Integer Prime Factorization","The Discrete Logarithm Problem (DLP) over finite groups","The Knapsack Problem","The Shortest Vector Problem (SVP)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Diffie-Hellman relies on the computational difficulty of extracting private exponent a from public value g^a mod p in a finite cyclic group."},{"question":"Why does TLS 1.3 mandate ephemeral key exchange (ECDHE) over static public keys?","options":["To guarantee Perfect Forward Secrecy (PFS), protecting past sessions if server private keys leak","To eliminate client-side key generation entirely","To reduce TLS handshake payloads down to 1 byte","To preserve backward compatibility with legacy Windows 98"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ECDHE generates fresh ephemeral keypairs per connection and discards private parameters upon termination, ensuring that compromising a server key cannot decrypt archived captures."}]},
  "crypto-03-02": {"html":"\n<h2>RSA Foundations & Euler's Totient Theorem</h2>\n\n<p>In 1977, Ron Rivest, Adi Shamir, and Leonard Adleman introduced the first <strong>practical asymmetric cryptosystem capable of both public-key encryption and digital signatures: RSA</strong>. The arrival of RSA permanently reshaped global digital commerce, identity validation, and modern network security.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive analogy: <strong>A Public Spring Padlock and an Exclusive Physical Key</strong>.\nIn symmetric encryption, distributing encrypted mail requires distributing copies of the single padlock key to millions of recipients—any single leaked key compromises all communications.\nRSA reverses this paradigm: You fabricate millions of <strong>open spring padlocks (Public Keys)</strong> and leave them openly available on street corners. Anyone wanting to send confidential mail puts their message into a sturdy lockbox, snaps the padlock shut with a click, and sends it to you. Once clicked shut, neither the sender nor any bystander can open it—only you, the sole possessor of the <strong>precision master key (Private Key)</strong>, can unlock it!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master one-way trapdoor functions and the Integer Prime Factorization Problem<br>\n  ② Deduce Euler's Totient Function \\(\\phi(n)\\) and Euler's Theorem (\\(a^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\pmod n\\))<br>\n  ③ Memorize the four-step RSA sequence: Key generation, encryption (\\(c = m^e \\bmod n\\)), and decryption (\\(m = c^d \\bmod n\\))<br>\n  ④ Apply the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to calculate modular multiplicative inverses</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: RSA Core Mathematical Framework Reference (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Sequence Step</th><th>Mathematical Operation</th><th>Description & Engineering Constraint</th><th>Exposure</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1. Select Two Primes</strong></td>\n      <td>Choose two large random primes (p) and (q)</td>\n      <td>Modern baseline requires 1024-bit primes minimum (yielding 2048-bit modulus)</td>\n      <td><strong>Strictly Confidential</strong></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>2. Compute Modulus (n)</strong></td>\n      <td>(n = p \times q)</td>\n      <td>The bit-length of (n) defines the RSA security key size (e.g., 2048 or 4096 bits)</td>\n      <td><strong>Completely Public</strong></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>3. Euler's Totient (phi(n))</strong></td>\n      <td>(phi(n) = (p - 1)(q - 1))</td>\n      <td>Number of coprimes below (n); secret trapdoor used to generate private exponents</td>\n      <td><strong>Strictly Confidential</strong></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>4. Select Public Exponent (e)</strong></td>\n      <td>(1 < e < phi(n)) such that (gcd(e, phi(n)) = 1)</td>\n      <td>Standard industry default is (e = 65537) ((2^{16} + 1), optimizes modular exponentiation)</td>\n      <td><strong>Completely Public</strong></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>5. Calculate Private Exponent (d)</strong></td>\n      <td>(e cdot d equiv 1 pmod{phi(n)}) 즉 (d = e^{-1} \bmod phi(n))</td>\n      <td>Computed via the Extended Euclidean Algorithm; required for decryption</td>\n      <td><strong>Strictly Confidential</strong></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>6. Encryption & Decryption</strong></td>\n      <td>Encrypt: (c = m^e \bmod n)<br>Decrypt: (m = c^d \bmod n)</td>\n      <td>Euler's theorem confirms: (c^d equiv (m^e)^d equiv m^{ed} equiv m^{kphi(n)+1} equiv m pmod n)</td>\n      <td>Symmetric inverse operations</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Pure Python Implementation of RSA Foundations</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n  <div class=\"copy-btn\">Copy</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\"># Pure Python implementation of RSA algebraic mechanics\nimport math\n\ndef extended_gcd(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Extended Euclidean algorithm: returns (gcd, x, y) for a*x + b*y = gcd\"\"\"\n    if b == 0:\n        return a, 1, 0\n    gcd, x1, y1 = extended_gcd(b, a % b)\n    return gcd, y1, x1 - (a // b) * y1\n\ndef mod_inverse(e, phi):\n    \"\"\"Computes modular inverse d such that (e * d) % phi == 1\"\"\"\n    gcd, x, _ = extended_gcd(e, phi)\n    if gcd != 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"e and phi must be coprime!\")\n    return x % phi\n\n# 1. Primes p and q (Miniature pedagogical parameters)\np, q = 61, 53\nn = p * q                      # n = 3233 (Modulus)\nphi = (p - 1) * (q - 1)        # phi = 60 * 52 = 3120 (Euler's Totient)\n\n# 2. Public exponent e\ne = 17\nassert math.gcd(e, phi) == 1, \"e must be coprime with phi\"\n\n# 3. Derive private exponent d\nd = mod_inverse(e, phi)       # d = 2753\nprint(f\"Public Key (e, n): ({e}, {n})\")\nprint(f\"Private Key (d, n): ({d}, {n})\")\n\n# 4. Encrypt message m (m < n)\nm = 65  # ASCII code for 'A'\nc = pow(m, e, n)\nprint(f\"Plaintext: {m} -> Encrypted Ciphertext: {c}\")\n\n# 5. Decrypt ciphertext back to m\ndecrypted_m = pow(c, d, n)\nprint(f\"Ciphertext: {c} -> Decrypted Plaintext: {decrypted_m}\")\nassert decrypted_m == m, \"Decryption must recover plaintext exactly!\"\nprint(\"✓ RSA algebraic mechanics validated successfully!\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercises:</strong><br>\n  Why is direct unpadded RSA (Textbook RSA: \\(c = m^e \\bmod n\\)) strictly prohibited in security engineering, and what standard padding mitigates its weaknesses?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> Textbook RSA is <strong>deterministic</strong>: An identical plaintext message \\(m\\) always yields identical ciphertext \\(c\\), allowing dictionary precomputation. Furthermore, it is mathematically <strong>malleable</strong>: Multiplying two ciphertexts yields \\(c_1 \\cdot c_2 \\equiv (m_1 m_2)^e \\pmod n\\), enabling ciphertext alterations without decryption. Secure production standards enforce <strong>RSA-OAEP (Optimal Asymmetric Encryption Padding)</strong>, introducing non-linear Feistel permutations and cryptographically secure random padding to guarantee semantic IND-CCA2 security.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① RSA relies upon the computational asymmetry between prime multiplication and integer factorization.<br>\n  ② Rigorous padding schemes (OAEP) are mandatory to transform pure modular math into secure cryptosystems.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In the RSA algorithm, given primes p = 61 and q = 53, what is the value of Euler's totient function φ(n)?","options":["3233","3120","3172","114"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Euler's totient φ(n) = (p - 1)(q - 1) = (61 - 1) * (53 - 1) = 60 * 52 = 3120."},{"question":"Which critical vulnerability affects unpadded Textbook RSA (c = m^e mod n)?","options":["Multiplicative malleability and deterministic ciphertext generation","Inability to execute on standard Turing machines","The requirement that public keys remain confidential","The mathematical condition that e must equal d"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Textbook RSA is deterministic (identical inputs yield identical outputs) and malleable (c1 * c2 mod n = (m1 * m2)^e mod n). Secure deployments mandate RSA-OAEP."}]},
  "crypto-03-03": {"html":"\n<h2>RSA Attack Vectors & CTF Challenges</h2>\n\n<p>In cybersecurity competitions (CTF) and adversarial engagements, the cryptography category is dominated by RSA challenge vectors. While RSA's mathematical architecture is elegant, slight engineering flaws in parameter generation, key reuse, or padding implementation can completely compromise the underlying cryptosystem in fractions of a second.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive analogy: <strong>Four Hidden Flaws in a Vault Door</strong>.\n① <strong>Small Exponent Broadcast Attack (Hastad's Broadcast)</strong>: If a server chooses (e=3) to optimize speed and sends identical content to three recipients, an attacker uses the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT) to compute the exact cube root over integers without factoring (n)!\n② <strong>Common Modulus Attack</strong>: If two employees share the same modulus (n) with different public exponents (e_1, e_2), capturing both encrypted messages allows immediate mathematical recovery using Bezout's identity!\n③ <strong>Fermat Factorization</strong>: If prime generation produces (p) and (q) too close to each other, Fermat's difference of squares algorithm factors (n) in microseconds!\n④ <strong>Wiener's Continued Fraction Attack</strong>: If a small private exponent (d < \frac{1}{3} n^{1/4}) is chosen to speed up decryption, continued fraction approximations recover (d) instantaneously!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Navigate the RSA cryptanalysis decision matrix based on observed parameter characteristics<br>\n  ② Execute Fermat factorization attacks against close prime distributions in Python<br>\n  ③ Implement Common Modulus attacks against shared modulus instances with coprime exponents<br>\n  ④ Exploit low public exponent broadcasts and Wiener's small private exponent vulnerabilities</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: RSA Cryptanalysis Decision Matrix (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Observed Flaw / Parameter Profile</th><th>Attack Methodology</th><th>Mathematical Principle</th><th>Complexity / Tool</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>(|p - q| < 2 n^{1/4}) (Primes too close)</td>\n      <td><strong>Fermat's Factorization</strong></td>\n      <td>Exploits (n = x^2 - y^2 = (x - y)(x + y)) by searching upward from (lceilsqrt{n}\rceil)</td>\n      <td>Milliseconds; minimal Python implementation</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Identical (n), distinct coprime (e_1, e_2)</td>\n      <td><strong>Common Modulus Attack</strong></td>\n      <td>Bezout identity yields (s_1 e_1 + s_2 e_2 = 1), leading to (c_1^{s_1} c_2^{s_2} equiv m pmod n)</td>\n      <td>Instantaneous via Extended Euclidean Algorithm</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>(e=3) or small, message (m) unpadded ((m^e < n))</td>\n      <td><strong>Direct Root Attack</strong></td>\n      <td>No modular wrap-around occurs; direct real (e)-th integer root extraction</td>\n      <td>No factorization needed; solved via <code>gmpy2.iroot(c, e)</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Small private exponent (d < \frac{1}{3} n^{1/4})</td>\n      <td><strong>Wiener's Continued Fractions</strong></td>\n      <td>Continued fraction convergents approximate (\frac{k}{d} approx \frac{e}{n})</td>\n      <td>Polynomial time instant recovery of private key (d)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>(p - 1) has only small prime factors (Smooth)</td>\n      <td><strong>Pollard's (p - 1) Attack</strong></td>\n      <td>Exploits Fermat's Little Theorem to extract (gcd(a^B - 1, n))</td>\n      <td>Seconds against non-safe prime moduli</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Python Script for Common Modulus Attacks</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n  <div class=\"copy-btn\">Copy</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\"># CTF Practical: Automated Common Modulus Attack script\nimport math\n\ndef extended_gcd(a, b):\n    if b == 0:\n        return a, 1, 0\n    gcd, x1, y1 = extended_gcd(b, a % b)\n    return gcd, y1, x1 - (a // b) * y1\n\ndef common_modulus_attack(n, e1, e2, c1, c2):\n    \"\"\"Recover plaintext m when encrypted under same n with coprime exponents e1, e2\"\"\"\n    gcd, s1, s2 = extended_gcd(e1, e2)\n    assert gcd == 1, \"e1 and e2 must be coprime!\"\n\n    # Handle negative exponents via modular inverse\n    if s1 < 0:\n        s1 = -s1\n        c1 = pow(c1, -1, n)\n    if s2 < 0:\n        s2 = -s2\n        c2 = pow(c2, -1, n)\n\n    # Compute m = (c1^s1 * c2^s2) % n\n    m = (pow(c1, s1, n) * pow(c2, s2, n)) % n\n    return m\n\n# Demonstration parameters\nn = 892384823705537\ne1, e2 = 17, 23\nsecret_m = 123456789\nc1 = pow(secret_m, e1, n)\nc2 = pow(secret_m, e2, n)\n\ncracked_m = common_modulus_attack(n, e1, e2, c1, c2)\nprint(f\"Target plaintext: {secret_m}\")\nprint(f\"Cracked plaintext: {cracked_m}\")\nassert cracked_m == secret_m, \"Attack must recover plaintext exactly!\"\nprint(\"✓ CTF Common Modulus exploitation validated successfully!\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercises:</strong><br>\n  In a CTF cryptography challenge, you are provided with public exponent \\(e = 65537\\) and a 2048-bit modulus \\(n\\). What initial reconnaissance steps should be executed before attempting complex mathematical attacks?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> First, query online factorization repositories such as <strong>factordb.com</strong> to verify whether (n) was previously factored or derived from vulnerable pseudo-random implementations. Second, if multiple public keys (n_1, n_2, dots) are provided in the problem set, immediately compute pairwise greatest common divisors (gcd(n_i, n_j)). If any prime factor (p) is accidentally reused across certificates, GCD extraction factors both moduli instantly without requiring trial division or lattice reduction.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Practical RSA security demands strict compliance with parameter generation guidelines to avoid low-exponent and close-prime traps.<br>\n  ② Never reuse moduli across distinct keypairs, and always enforce certified padding schemes.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In CTF competitions, when two recipients encrypt identical plaintext using shared modulus n and coprime exponents e1, e2, which attack applies?","options":["Common Modulus Attack","Wiener's Continued Fraction Attack","Fermat's Factorization","Hastad's Broadcast Attack"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When gcd(e1, e2) = 1, Bezout's identity provides coefficients s1*e1 + s2*e2 = 1. Evaluating (c1^s1 * c2^s2) mod n recovers the plaintext without factoring n."},{"question":"When RSA prime factors p and q are generated unusually close to each other (|p - q| is small), which factorization algorithm breaks n rapidly?","options":["Fermat's Factorization","Shor's Algorithm","Birthday Attack","Rainbow table brute-forcing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Fermat's factorization expresses n as difference of squares n = x^2 - y^2. When p and q are close, x is marginally larger than sqrt(n), finding integer roots in milliseconds."}]},
  "crypto-04-01": {"html":"\n<h2>Hash Families: MD5, SHA-2 & SHA-3</h2>\n\n<p>Cryptographic hash functions operate as the digital DNA extractors of modern information security. They transform arbitrary-length input data into fixed-size cryptographic digests through irreversible compression operations. An ideal cryptographic hash function acts like a biometric fingerprint: Unique, unforgeable, and exhibiting a dramatic <strong>Avalanche Effect</strong> upon any minuscule variation in input data.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive analogy: <strong>The Digital Fingerprint Press</strong>.\nWhether feeding in a single grain of rice (1 byte), an entire multi-volume encyclopedia (millions of words), or an ultra-high-definition 4K video (tens of gigabytes), the press extracts an invariant 256-bit identifier (SHA-256). Changing even a solitary comma to a period causes approximately 50% of the 256 output bits to flip instantaneously! One cannot reverse the crushed fingerprint back into the original video (Pre-image Resistance), nor can one fabricate two distinct files that yield identical prints (Collision Resistance).</p>\n\n<div id=\"sandbox-hash\" class=\"sandbox-container\">\n  <!-- SHA-256 hash calculation and real-time avalanche effect micro-sandbox mounted by frontend -->\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master the three fundamental security properties: Pre-image, Second Pre-image, and Collision Resistance<br>\n  ② Analyze Merkle-Damgård iterative compression versus Keccak's sponge construction<br>\n  ③ Review the historic collapse of MD5 and SHA-1 under the Birthday Paradox bound<br>\n  ④ Measure avalanche bit-flip ratios and perform data integrity verification in Python</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Cryptographic Hash Families Comparative Reference (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Algorithm Family</th><th>Digest Bit-Length</th><th>Underlying Architecture</th><th>Collision Resistance</th><th>Status & Production Guidance</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>MD5</strong></td>\n      <td>128 bits (16 bytes)</td>\n      <td>Merkle-Damgård construction</td>\n      <td><strong>Completely Broken</strong> (Wang et al., 2004)</td>\n      <td><strong>Prohibited in security</strong>; non-adversarial checksums only</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>SHA-1</strong></td>\n      <td>160 bits (20 bytes)</td>\n      <td>Merkle-Damgård construction</td>\n      <td><strong>Broken</strong> (Google SHAttered, 2017)</td>\n      <td><strong>Globally Deprecated</strong>; rejected by modern TLS and browsers</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>SHA-2 (SHA-256/512)</strong></td>\n      <td>256 / 384 / 512 bits</td>\n      <td>Merkle-Damgård construction</td>\n      <td><strong>Exceptional</strong> ((2^{128}) collision complexity)</td>\n      <td><strong>Global Industry Standard</strong>; ubiquitous in TLS, PKI, and Bitcoin</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>SHA-3 (Keccak)</strong></td>\n      <td>224 / 256 / 384 / 512 bits</td>\n      <td><strong>Sponge Construction</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>Exceptional</strong> (Immune to length extension attacks)</td>\n      <td><strong>Next-Gen Standard</strong>; EVM Ethereum core standard</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>BLAKE3</strong></td>\n      <td>256 bits (Configurable)</td>\n      <td>Tree hashing structure</td>\n      <td><strong>Exceptional</strong></td>\n      <td>High-throughput computing; orders of magnitude faster than SHA-256</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: The Three Core Cryptographic Properties & Birthday Bound</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Security Property</th><th>Mathematical Definition</th><th>Plain English Intuition</th><th>Brute-force Complexity</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Pre-image Resistance<br>(One-Wayness)</strong></td>\n      <td>Given hash (h), finding any (m) such that (H(m) = h) is computationally infeasible</td>\n      <td>Given a fingerprint, cannot reconstruct the suspect's face</td>\n      <td>Search space of (2^n) operations ((2^{256}) for SHA-256)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Second Pre-image Resistance<br>(Weak Collision)</strong></td>\n      <td>Given message (m_1), finding (m_2 \neq m_1) such that (H(m_1) = H(m_2)) is infeasible</td>\n      <td>Given a valid contract, cannot craft a counterfeit with identical hash</td>\n      <td>Search space of (2^n) operations</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Collision Resistance<br>(Strong Collision)</strong></td>\n      <td>Finding any pair (m_1 \neq m_2) such that (H(m_1) = H(m_2)) is infeasible</td>\n      <td>Finding any two arbitrary documents that share identical hash</td>\n      <td><strong>Halved to (2^{n/2}) by Birthday Paradox!</strong> (MD5 crushed in (2^{64}) operations)</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Measuring Hash Avalanche Effect in Python</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n  <div class=\"copy-btn\">Copy</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\"># Demonstrating SHA-256 Avalanche Effect in Python\nimport hashlib\n\ndef sha256_bits(text):\n    \"\"\"Convert input string to 256-bit binary representation\"\"\"\n    digest_bytes = hashlib.sha256(text.encode('utf-8')).digest()\n    return ''.join(f'{b:08b}' for b in digest_bytes)\n\nmsg1 = \"Transfer $1,000 to Bob.\"\nmsg2 = \"Transfer $1,000 to Bob!\"   # Replacing single punctuation mark\n\nbits1 = sha256_bits(msg1)\nbits2 = sha256_bits(msg2)\n\nflipped_bits = sum(b1 != b2 for b1, b2 in zip(bits1, bits2))\navalanche_ratio = (flipped_bits / 256) * 100\n\nprint(f\"Original digest: {hashlib.sha256(msg1.encode()).hexdigest()}\")\nprint(f\"Modified digest: {hashlib.sha256(msg2.encode()).hexdigest()}\")\nprint(f\"Flipped {flipped_bits}/256 bits (Avalanche ratio: {avalanche_ratio:.2f}%)\")\nassert 40 <= avalanche_ratio <= 60, \"Avalanche flip ratio must hover around 50%!\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercises:</strong><br>\n  Why does an attacker targeting an MD5-based integrity verification system not need to break pre-image resistance, and how does a chosen-prefix collision succeed instead?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> Breaking MD5 pre-image resistance requires (2^{128}) operations, which remains prohibitive. However, using <strong>Chosen-Prefix Collision</strong> attacks, adversaries precalculate two distinct documents—one benign and one containing unauthorized code—that evaluate to identical MD5 checksums within (2^{39}) operations. Once the benign version is authorized and signed, swapping it with the malicious variant preserves the cryptographic signature. All modern integrity systems must enforce SHA-256 or SHA-3.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① The Birthday Paradox reduces collision search spaces to \\(2^{n/2}\\).<br>\n  ② MD5 and SHA-1 must be purged from all security perimeters in favor of SHA-256 or Keccak/SHA-3.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Due to the Birthday Paradox, what is the computational complexity required to find a collision in an n-bit cryptographic hash?","options":["2^n operations","2^(n/2) operations","n^2 operations","2^(n/4) operations"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Birthday Paradox slashes strong collision resistance from 2^n down to 2^(n/2). Hence, a 128-bit hash like MD5 requires merely 2^64 operations to collide."},{"question":"What architectural departure distinguishes SHA-3 (Keccak) from legacy SHA-2 designs?","options":["Adoption of the Sponge Construction","Utilization of Feistel networks","Use of the Merkle-Damgård iterative compression loop","Application of affine matrix ciphers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"SHA-3 abandons Merkle-Damgård architectures in favor of a wide-state Sponge construction (absorbing and squeezing phases), naturally immune to length extension."}]},
  "crypto-04-02": {"html":"\n<h2>Length Extension Attack & HMAC Message Auth</h2>\n\n<p>A frequent error among software architects designing API authentication tokens is employing intuitive constructions like <code>token = Hash(secret + data)</code>. The intuition suggests that because the client never learns <code>secret</code>, tampering with <code>data</code> will invalidate the signature. This widespread design flaw introduces one of cryptography's most subtle yet devastating exploits: <strong>Hash Length Extension Attacks</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive analogy: <strong>Appending Unauthorized Clauses to an Authenticated Scroll</strong>.\nAll Merkle-Damgård based hash functions (MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256) operate like an iterative compression machine: Data is processed in 64-byte blocks, with each iteration updating an internal register state. When a server issues <code>H(secret + \"user=guest\")</code>, the resulting hash exposes <strong>the exact final register state of that compression machine</strong>!\nAn attacker does not need the secret: They simply initialize a new hash machine with that captured state, pad the original message properly, and continue hashing additional unauthorized parameters like <code>\"&role=admin\"</code>!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Analyze Merkle-Damgård iterative compression states and padding mechanics<br>\n  ② Execute Hash Length Extension attacks using captured hash states and synthetic padding<br>\n  ③ Master the architecture of Keyed-Hash Message Authentication Codes (HMAC, RFC 2104)<br>\n  ④ Understand how dual-layer XOR padding (ipad and opad) eliminates length extension vulnerabilities</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Insecure Prefix Hashing vs HMAC Architecture (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Design Pattern</th><th>Mathematical Formula</th><th>Length Extension Immunity</th><th>Security Posture</th><th>Recommended Usage</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Secret-Prefix Hashing (Flawed)</strong></td>\n      <td>(\text{Token} = H(\text{secret} | \text{message}))</td>\n      <td><strong>Completely Vulnerable</strong> (MD5/SHA-1/SHA-256 all exploitable)</td>\n      <td>High-Severity Flaw</td>\n      <td>Strictly prohibited; frequent CTF vulnerability</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Secret-Suffix Hashing (Flawed)</strong></td>\n      <td>(\text{Token} = H(\text{message} | \text{secret}))</td>\n      <td>Immune to extension, but vulnerable to collisions</td>\n      <td>Non-Standard</td>\n      <td>Discouraged; non-standardized primitive</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Standard HMAC (RFC 2104)</strong></td>\n      <td>(H((K oplus \text{opad}) | H((K oplus \text{ipad}) | m)))</td>\n      <td><strong>100% Immune</strong> (Nested hashing breaks state continuity)</td>\n      <td><strong>Global Gold Standard</strong></td>\n      <td>Universal standard for API auth, JWT signatures, and TLS</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>KMAC (SHA-3 Based)</strong></td>\n      <td>(\text{KMAC}(\text{key}, \text{data})) based on cSHAKE</td>\n      <td><strong>Inherently Immune</strong> (Sponge construction lacks iterative registers)</td>\n      <td>Post-Quantum Standard</td>\n      <td>Modern high-performance cryptographic standard</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Step-by-Step HMAC Protocol Execution</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Protocol Stage</th><th>Mathematical Execution</th><th>Description</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1. Key Normalization</strong></td>\n      <td>If (\text{len}(K) > B), (K' = H(K)); else pad with zero-bytes to block length (B) (64 bytes)</td>\n      <td>Aligns key size to the underlying hash block boundary</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>2. Inner Digest Stage</strong></td>\n      <td>(S_{in} = K' oplus \text{ipad})<br>(H_{inner} = H(S_{in} | \text{message}))</td>\n      <td>(\text{ipad}) repeated constant <code>0x36</code>; combines key with payload</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>3. Outer Digest Stage</strong></td>\n      <td>(S_{out} = K' oplus \text{opad})<br>(\text{HMAC} = H(S_{out} | H_{inner}))</td>\n      <td>(\text{opad}) repeated constant <code>0x5c</code>; hashes inner digest, hiding internal states</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 3: RFC 2104 HMAC Implementation in Pure Python</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n  <div class=\"copy-btn\">Copy</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\"># Pure Python implementation of RFC 2104 HMAC-SHA256\nimport hashlib\nimport hmac\n\ndef custom_hmac_sha256(key: bytes, msg: bytes) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Manual RFC 2104 HMAC implementation\"\"\"\n    block_size = 64\n    \n    # 1. Normalize key length\n    if len(key) > block_size:\n        key = hashlib.sha256(key).digest()\n    key = key.ljust(block_size, b'\\x00')\n    \n    # 2. Inner & outer pad constants\n    ipad = bytes([0x36] * block_size)\n    opad = bytes([0x5c] * block_size)\n    \n    # 3. Inner hash\n    inner_key = bytes(k ^ p for k, p in zip(key, ipad))\n    inner_hash = hashlib.sha256(inner_key + msg).digest()\n    \n    # 4. Outer hash\n    outer_key = bytes(k ^ p for k, p in zip(key, opad))\n    outer_hash = hashlib.sha256(outer_key + inner_hash).hexdigest()\n    \n    return outer_hash\n\n# Verification against standard library\nsecret = b\"my_super_secret_key\"\ndata = b\"action=transfer&amount=5000&to=bob\"\n\nmy_result = custom_hmac_sha256(secret, data)\nstd_result = hmac.new(secret, data, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\n\nprint(f\"Custom HMAC:   {my_result}\")\nprint(f\"Standard HMAC: {std_result}\")\nassert my_result == std_result, \"Custom implementation must match RFC 2104!\"\nprint(\"✓ HMAC verified: Dual-layer hashing eliminates length extension attacks!\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercises:</strong><br>\n  Why does the Keccak (SHA-3) family not require the complex dual-pass HMAC wrapper to prevent length extension attacks?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> Merkle-Damgård hashes expose their entire internal state in the output digest, allowing attackers to continue the compression chain. Keccak utilizes a <strong>Sponge Construction</strong> with a wide internal state (1600 bits) comprising both bitrate (r) and capacity (c). During the squeezing phase, only a portion of the state is revealed, while the internal capacity bits remain completely hidden. Because an attacker cannot observe or reconstruct the unexposed capacity bits, length extension is mathematically impossible, enabling direct keyed hashing via KMAC.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Never implement secret-prefix hashing \\(H(secret | data)\\) in authentication APIs.<br>\n  ② Always adopt standardized HMAC (RFC 2104) or SHA-3 KMAC constructions.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Why is an authentication token structured as Token = Hash(secret + message) fundamentally flawed?","options":["Adversaries can exploit the exposed Merkle-Damgård register state to conduct Length Extension attacks","The secret is transmitted in cleartext across the network","It cannot be computed on mobile devices","It fails to mitigate replay attacks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Merkle-Damgård digests expose internal compression states. Attackers can append malicious data to the padded message and resume hashing without knowing the secret."},{"question":"How does standard HMAC (RFC 2104) eliminate hash length extension attacks?","options":["By enforcing dual-pass nested hashing: H((K ⊕ opad) || H((K ⊕ ipad) || m))","By truncating plaintexts to under 16 bytes","By introducing RSA asymmetric signatures during handshakes","By converting outputs to Base64"],"answer":0,"explanation":"HMAC applies inner (ipad 0x36) and outer (opad 0x5c) XOR key pads. The inner digest is wrapped and re-hashed by the outer layer, concealing internal register states."}]},
  "crypto-04-03": {"html":"\n<h2>Secure Password Hashing: bcrypt & Argon2</h2>\n\n<p>Every year, enterprise data breaches expose hundreds of millions of user credentials. Storing credentials in plaintext or with standard general-purpose hashes (such as MD5 or single-pass SHA-256) enables adversaries wielding modern GPU clusters and precomputed rainbow tables to reverse over 90% of user passwords within minutes. Modern authentication requires specialized <strong>slow, memory-hard key derivation functions</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive analogy: <strong>The Triple-Barrier Defense for User Passwords</strong>.\n① <strong>Barrier 1: Unique Salt (Anti-Rainbow Table)</strong>: Adding a unique cryptographic salt per user ensures that even if ten million users pick <code>Password123</code>, every single generated hash is entirely distinct, rendering multi-terabyte precomputed rainbow tables useless.\n② <strong>Barrier 2: Work Factors (Slow Hashes)</strong>: Fast algorithms like SHA-256 allow modern GPUs to evaluate billions of attempts per second. Slow hashes deliberately iterate thousands of times, elevating per-hash compute times to 100–300 milliseconds. For an individual login, 0.1s is imperceptible; for an adversary attempting billions of hashes, it extends cracking time from days to centuries.\n③ <strong>Barrier 3: Memory Hardness (Anti-ASIC)</strong>: Custom cracking rigs employ ASICs and FPGAs with immense raw compute but limited on-die cache. Modern champions like <strong>Argon2id</strong> mandate allocations of dozens of megabytes of RAM during evaluation, choking parallelized ASIC hardware.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand why general-purpose hashes (MD5, SHA-256) are dangerous for password storage<br>\n  ② Analyze rainbow table precomputations and evaluate the protective role of unique per-user salts<br>\n  ③ Compare the modern key derivation lineage: PBKDF2, bcrypt, scrypt, and Argon2id<br>\n  ④ Configure cost factors, memory hardness, and constant-time verification routines in production</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Password Storage KDF Evolution Reference (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Core Mechanism</th><th>GPU Resistance</th><th>ASIC / FPGA Resistance</th><th>Current Industry Recommendation</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Single-pass SHA-256</strong></td>\n      <td>High-speed general digest</td>\n      <td><strong>None</strong> (Billions of hashes/sec per GPU)</td>\n      <td><strong>None</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>Strictly Prohibited</strong> (Even with salt)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>PBKDF2 (RFC 2898)</strong></td>\n      <td>HMAC iterated 600,000+ rounds</td>\n      <td>Moderate</td>\n      <td>Low (ASIC easily parallelizes simple compute)</td>\n      <td>Legacy baseline (Minimal compliance only)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>bcrypt (Eksblowfish)</strong></td>\n      <td>Blowfish schedule with adjustable work factor</td>\n      <td><strong>Strong</strong> (Demands on-chip cache)</td>\n      <td>Good</td>\n      <td><strong>Industry Standard</strong> (Widely deployed baseline)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>scrypt</strong></td>\n      <td>Sequential memory-hard architecture</td>\n      <td><strong>Very High</strong></td>\n      <td>Strong (Inflates silicon footprint dramatically)</td>\n      <td>Excellent (Prevalent in blockchain storage)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Argon2id</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>PHC Winner</strong>; hybrid memory-hard & side-channel defense</td>\n      <td><strong>Exceptional</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>Exceptional</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>Gold Standard</strong> (Recommended for all new systems)</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Anatomy of a Standard bcrypt String</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n  <div class=\"copy-btn\">Copy</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Standard bcrypt format:\n$2b$12$e8Y7a2q4M.K6aZ1i0PqL0uWvG3H6F8jL2M4N6P8R0T2V4X6Z8A0B2\n__/ / ____________________/_____________________________/\n |    |            |                         |\nIdentifier Cost (12)    128-bit Salt (22 chars)     184-bit Hash (31 chars)\n (2^12 iterations)      (Base64 encoded)           (Blowfish ciphertext)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Secure Password Hashing in Python with bcrypt</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n  <div class=\"copy-btn\">Copy</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\"># Production-ready password hashing with Python bcrypt\nimport bcrypt\nimport time\n\nuser_password = b\"SuperP@ssw0rd!2026\"\n\n# 1. Generate salt and hash with cost factor 12 (2^12 = 4096 rounds)\nstart_time = time.time()\nsalt = bcrypt.gensalt(rounds=12)\nhashed = bcrypt.hashpw(user_password, salt)\nelapsed = time.time() - start_time\n\nprint(f\"Generated bcrypt hash: {hashed.decode('utf-8')}\")\nprint(f\"Hashing duration: {elapsed:.3f}s (Aim for 0.1-0.3s per authentication)\")\n\n# 2. Credential verification\ncandidate_pass = b\"SuperP@ssw0rd!2026\"\ninvalid_pass = b\"WrongPassword123\"\n\n# bcrypt.checkpw extracts the embedded salt and executes constant-time comparison\nassert bcrypt.checkpw(candidate_pass, hashed) is True, \"Valid password must verify!\"\nassert bcrypt.checkpw(invalid_pass, hashed) is False, \"Invalid password must fail!\"\nprint(\"✓ Credential verification succeeded with zero timing leakage!\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercises:</strong><br>\n  Why does conventional string equality comparison (<code>input_hash == stored_hash</code>) introduce high-risk <strong>Timing Attacks</strong>, and how do cryptographic libraries resolve it?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> Standard string equality operators employ early exit: Comparison terminates at the first non-matching byte. Measuring microsecond latency variations allows an adversary to deduce matching characters sequentially without brute-forcing the whole token. Cryptographic engines mandate <strong>Constant-Time Comparison routines (e.g., <code>hmac.compare_digest</code>)</strong>, which evaluate every byte unconditionally across the entire array, completely flattening latency differentials.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Password storage requires unique random salts, calibrated work factors, and constant-time comparison.<br>\n  ② Modern architectures should mandate Argon2id or bcrypt over legacy SHA digests.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Why must high-speed general-purpose hashes (e.g., MD5 or uniterated SHA-256) never be used for password storage?","options":["Modern GPUs evaluate billions of general hashes per second, enabling rapid rainbow table reversing","SHA-256 contains a mathematical backdoor permitting direct inversion","SHA-256 binaries cannot compile on Linux kernels","MD5 outputs exceed standard database column limits"],"answer":0,"explanation":"General-purpose digests optimize for throughput, allowing parallel hardware to test billions of guesses per second. Credential storage requires slow, memory-hard functions like bcrypt or Argon2id."},{"question":"Which algorithm triumphed in the Password Hashing Competition (PHC) and represents modern gold-standard credential storage?","options":["Argon2 (specifically Argon2id)","MD6","DES-Crypt","NTLM"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Argon2 won the Password Hashing Competition. Its hybrid Argon2id variant defends simultaneously against GPU/ASIC parallelization and side-channel timing attacks."}]},
  "crypto-05-01": {"html":"\n<h2>Digital Signature Schemes: RSA-PSS & ECDSA</h2>\n\n<p>In physical contracts, wet signatures and wax seals attest to authorization and agreement. In the digital landscape where bitwise duplication is effortless, ensuring <strong>Authentication</strong>, <strong>Integrity</strong>, and <strong>Non-repudiation</strong> demands mathematical guarantees. <strong>Digital Signatures</strong> provide the unforgeable mathematical proofs securing electronic contracts, code signing, and decentralized financial ledgers.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive analogy: <strong>The Imperial Wax Seal & Verification Mold</strong>.\nWhen an emperor drafts an imperial decree, the court scribe computes a 256-bit cryptographic digest. The emperor applies their exclusive, highly guarded <strong>Signet Seal (Private Key)</strong> over that digest, producing an intricate wax impression (the digital signature).\nCitizens receive identical copies of a rigid <strong>Verification Mold (Public Key)</strong>. Anyone can press their mold against the wax impression: If it mates seamlessly and the document's computed hash matches the stamped fingerprint, citizens gain 100% mathematical assurance that the decree originated exclusively from the emperor and remained untampered in transit.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master the core digital signature loop: Private key signing and public key mathematical verification<br>\n  ② Evaluate RSA-PSS probabilistic padding versus legacy deterministic PKCS#1 v1.5 schemes<br>\n  ③ Analyze the compact efficiency of Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithms (ECDSA & Ed25519)<br>\n  ④ Deconstruct the historic Sony PlayStation 3 ECDSA nonce reuse catastrophe</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Digital Signature Standards Comparison (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Algorithm Standard</th><th>Mathematical Basis</th><th>Key/Signature Size (128-bit Security)</th><th>Nonce Dependency & Risk</th><th>Industry Adoption & Compliance</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>RSA PKCS#1 v1.5</strong></td>\n      <td>Integer Prime Factorization</td>\n      <td>2048-bit modulus (256 bytes)</td>\n      <td>Deterministic padding; vulnerable to Bleichenbacher Bleichenbacher attacks</td>\n      <td>Legacy baseline; universally scheduled for deprecation</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>RSA-PSS</strong></td>\n      <td>Integer Factorization + Probabilistic Salt</td>\n      <td>2048-bit modulus (256 bytes)</td>\n      <td>Incorporates random salt; proven provable security bound</td>\n      <td><strong>Modern RSA Gold Standard</strong>; mandatory in TLS 1.3</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>ECDSA (secp256r1/k1)</strong></td>\n      <td>Elliptic Curve Discrete Logarithm</td>\n      <td>Public key <strong>only 64 bytes</strong>, signature 64 bytes</td>\n      <td><strong>Critically dependent on entropy (k)</strong>; nonce reuse leaks private key!</td>\n      <td>Universal standard in TLS 1.2/1.3 and Bitcoin/Ethereum</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Ed25519 (EdDSA)</strong></td>\n      <td>Twisted Edwards Curve (Curve25519)</td>\n      <td>Public key <strong>only 32 bytes</strong>, signature 64 bytes</td>\n      <td><strong>Deterministic derivation of (k)</strong>; immune to RNG failure and side channels</td>\n      <td><strong>Next-Gen Champion</strong>; default in modern SSH, Signal, and Git signing</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: ECDSA Signature Sequence & The Nonce Reuse Catastrophe</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Phase</th><th>Mathematical Formula</th><th>Operational Details</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1. Signature Generation</strong></td>\n      <td>Select random integer (k); compute point ((x_1, y_1) = k cdot G)<br>(r = x_1 \bmod n)<br>(s = k^{-1} (H(m) + d_A cdot r) \bmod n)</td>\n      <td>Output signature pair ((r, s)) where (d_A) is private key. <strong>Crucial</strong>: Nonce (k) must be unpredictable and unique per signature!</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>2. Verification</strong></td>\n      <td>(w = s^{-1} \bmod n)<br>(u_1 = H(m) cdot w \bmod n, u_2 = r cdot w \bmod n)<br>Point (P = u_1 cdot G + u_2 cdot Q_A)</td>\n      <td>Valid if and only if (x_P equiv r pmod n).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>3. Nonce Reuse Exploit<br>(Sony PS3 Breach)</strong></td>\n      <td>If identical nonce (k) signs two messages (m_1, m_2):<br>(s_1 - s_2 = k^{-1}(H(m_1) - H(m_2)) implies k = \frac{H(m_1) - H(m_2)}{s_1 - s_2})<br>Private key: (d_A = \frac{s_1 k - H(m_1)}{r})</td>\n      <td><strong>Solved with elementary algebra!</strong> In 2010, hackers observed Sony's PS3 bootloader firmware static hardcoded (k), instantly extracting Sony's master private root key.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Implementing Ed25519 Signatures in Python</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n  <div class=\"copy-btn\">Copy</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\"># Modern Ed25519 digital signature workflow in Python\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric import ed25519\nfrom cryptography.exceptions import InvalidSignature\n\n# 1. Generate Ed25519 keypair in microseconds\nprivate_key = ed25519.Ed25519PrivateKey.generate()\npublic_key = private_key.public_key()\n\n# 2. Sign confidential transaction document\ndocument = b\"CONTRACT: Transfer 50.0 BTC to 1BoatSLRHtKNngkdXEeobR76b53LETtpyT\"\nsignature = private_key.sign(document)\n\nprint(f\"Generated Ed25519 signature size: {len(signature)} bytes\")\nprint(f\"Hex signature: {signature.hex()[:60]}...\")\n\n# 3. Recipient verifies validity using sender's public key\ntry:\n    public_key.verify(signature, document)\n    print(\"✓ Signature verified: Document authentic and untampered!\")\nexcept InvalidSignature:\n    print(\"✗ Invalid signature!\")\n\n# 4. Tampering detection simulation\ntampered = b\"CONTRACT: Transfer 999 BTC to Adversary\"\ntry:\n    public_key.verify(signature, tampered)\n    print(\"✗ Tampered document verified (Critical flaw)\")\nexcept InvalidSignature:\n    print(\"✓ Anti-tampering confirmed: Signature verification rejected forged payload!\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercises:</strong><br>\n  Engineers frequently conflate asymmetric encryption with digital signing. How are the private and public keys reversed across these two operations?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n  • <strong>Public Key Encryption (Confidentiality)</strong>: Anyone encrypts using your <strong>Public Key</strong>; only you decrypt with your exclusive <strong>Private Key</strong>.<br>\n  • <strong>Digital Signatures (Authentication & Non-repudiation)</strong>: Only you sign using your exclusive <strong>Private Key</strong>; anyone verifies using your openly known <strong>Public Key</strong>.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Signatures operate over cryptographic digests rather than monolithic message payloads.<br>\n  ② Prefer Ed25519 or RSA-PSS over legacy deterministic PKCS#1 v1.5 constructions.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"How do key roles invert between asymmetric encryption and digital signatures?","options":["Signatures use the sender's Private Key to sign and Public Key to verify; encryption uses Public to encrypt and Private to decrypt","Signatures use Public Keys to sign and Private Keys to verify","Both parties rely on pre-shared symmetric keys","The receiver generates the signature and transmits it backward"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Digital signing requires the exclusive private key to attest identity, while anyone can verify the proof using the signer's known public key."},{"question":"What implementation failure allowed hackers to derive Sony's master private key in the PS3 bootloader breach?","options":["Reusing the identical nonce k across ECDSA signatures","Using 512-bit RSA keys","A SQL injection in Sony update servers","Omitting firmware checksums"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ECDSA mandates unique, unpredictable nonces k. Sony hardcoded a static k, allowing hackers to isolate and extract the master private root key via simple subtraction."}]},
  "crypto-05-02": {"html":"\n<h2>X.509 Certificates & Chain of Trust</h2>\n\n<p>Digital signatures ensure that anyone holding a genuine public key can verify authenticity. However, this introduces an immediate philosophical conundrum: <strong>How do you prove that a received public key actually belongs to the intended domain rather than an active impersonator?</strong> If an adversary injects their own public key into an unauthenticated stream claiming to be <code>google.com</code>, you would encrypt data under the adversary's key. The <strong>Public Key Infrastructure (PKI)</strong> and the <strong>X.509 Standard</strong> solve this identity bind.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive analogy: <strong>The Hierarchy of Notarized Passports</strong>.\n① <strong>Root Certificate Authority (Root CA)</strong>: Functions like a nation's supreme constitutional court. Modern operating systems and browsers ship with approximately 100 pre-installed self-signed Root CA certificates, acting as the absolute bedrock of global digital trust.\n② <strong>Intermediate CA</strong>: Root CAs remain strictly offline in high-security air-gapped vaults. To handle routine issuance, their offline private keys sign certificates delegating authority to operational Intermediate CAs.\n③ <strong>Leaf Server Certificate</strong>: When hosting an enterprise domain (e.g., <code>example.com</code>), you prove domain ownership to an Intermediate CA, which signs an X.509 certificate packaging your public key and domain metadata.\nClients navigate this <strong>Chain of Trust</strong> recursively up to pre-installed root certificates, validating every intermediate signature.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Deconstruct X.509 v3 architecture (Subject, Issuer, SAN, Validity, and Extensions)<br>\n  ② Trace recursive cryptographic validation across the PKI Chain of Trust to trust anchors<br>\n  ③ Evaluate certificate revocation paradigms: CRL distribution, real-time OCSP, and OCSP Stapling<br>\n  ④ Execute OpenSSL inspection commands to analyze ASN.1 structures and public key fingerprints</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: X.509 Core Architectural Attributes (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Field Name</th><th>OID Identifier</th><th>Technical Description</th><th>Security Implication</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Version</strong></td>\n      <td>v3 (Integer 2)</td>\n      <td>Universal modern baseline supporting custom extension sets</td>\n      <td>Legacy v1 certificates lack SAN support and are rejected by browsers</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Subject</strong></td>\n      <td><code>C=US, O=Corp, CN=domain</code></td>\n      <td>Distinguished Name (DN) identifying certificate owner</td>\n      <td>Common Name (CN) is deprecated; SAN is authoritative</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Issuer</strong></td>\n      <td><code>CN=DigiCert Global Root</code></td>\n      <td>Distinguished Name of issuing Certificate Authority</td>\n      <td>Root CA certificates are self-signed (Issuer equals Subject)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Subject Alternative Name</strong></td>\n      <td><code>subjectAltName (SAN)</code></td>\n      <td><strong>Authoritative domain matching list</strong>; supports wildcards (<code>*.example.com</code>)</td>\n      <td>Mismatched domains trigger <code>NET::ERR_CERT_COMMON_NAME_INVALID</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Basic Constraints</strong></td>\n      <td><code>basicConstraints</code></td>\n      <td><strong>Critical extension</strong> designating CA status (<code>CA:TRUE</code> or <code>CA:FALSE</code>)</td>\n      <td>End-entity certificates must enforce <code>CA:FALSE</code> to prevent unauthorized signing</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Validity Period</strong></td>\n      <td><code>Not Before</code> / <code>Not After</code></td>\n      <td>Valid UTC timeframe (industry limits max lifetime to 398 days)</td>\n      <td>Expiration triggers <code>NET::ERR_CERT_DATE_INVALID</code> warnings</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Certificate Revocation Approaches Compared</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Revocation Technique</th><th>Operational Mechanism</th><th>Latency & Privacy Considerations</th><th>Security Profile</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>CRL (Revocation Lists)</strong></td>\n      <td>Clients download periodic monolithic lists of revoked serial numbers from CA</td>\n      <td>Files expand to tens of megabytes; high latency and bandwidth overhead</td>\n      <td>Obsolete</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>OCSP (Online Protocol)</strong></td>\n      <td>Client sends real-time HTTP queries to CA OCSP servers per certificate</td>\n      <td><strong>Leaks browsing history to CA</strong>; introduces noticeable handshake latency</td>\n      <td>Vulnerable to soft-fail bypass</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>OCSP Stapling</strong></td>\n      <td>Web server caches signed OCSP response from CA and staples it directly to TLS handshake</td>\n      <td><strong>Optimal performance and absolute privacy</strong>; zero client query latency</td>\n      <td><strong>Production Best Practice</strong></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Practical Certificate Auditing with OpenSSL</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n  <div class=\"copy-btn\">Copy</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># 1. Fetch full certificate chain from remote endpoint\nopenssl s_client -connect example.com:443 -showcerts &lt; /dev/null\n\n# 2. Parse complete ASN.1 structure and extensions of local certificate\nopenssl x509 -in cert.pem -text -noout\n\n# 3. Extract core identity metadata: Issuer, Validity, and SAN domains\nopenssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -issuer -dates -ext subjectAltName\n\n# 4. Generate SHA-256 fingerprint for certificate verification\nopenssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -fingerprint -sha256</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercises:</strong><br>\n  Why does disabling certificate validation (e.g., <code>curl -k</code> or <code>verify=False</code>) completely negate HTTPS protection, and how do enterprise networks resolve private testing needs safely?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> Disabling validation instructs the client to accept any presented certificate blindly. Any local adversary conducting ARP poisoning or hosting rogue Wi-Fi can present self-signed certificates, intercepting and decrypting confidential traffic without warning. Enterprises must establish an <strong>internal Private PKI (such as HashiCorp Vault) and distribute corporate private Root CA certificates into managed endpoint truststores</strong>, ensuring full cryptographic verification without bypassing security perimeters.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① X.509 chains anchor trust via pre-installed root authorities, validating identity across intermediate links.<br>\n  ② Production infrastructure should configure OCSP Stapling and strictly prohibit client validation overrides.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Which X.509 certificate extension is authoritative for domain name verification in modern web browsers?","options":["Subject Alternative Name (SAN)","Common Name (CN)","Country Code (C)","Organization (O)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The legacy Common Name (CN) field is officially deprecated. Modern browsers enforce hostname matching exclusively against the Subject Alternative Name (SAN) extension."},{"question":"What primary operational benefit does OCSP Stapling provide over standard OCSP queries?","options":["The web server caches and staples signed CA revocation tokens directly into TLS handshakes, optimizing speed and preserving privacy","It eliminates the need for Certificate Authorities","It revokes decommissioned root certificates permanently","It allows clients to bypass certificate validation entirely"],"answer":0,"explanation":"OCSP queries leak browsing traffic to CAs and add connection latency. OCSP Stapling lets servers retrieve valid proofs periodically and attach them directly to client handshakes."}]},
  "crypto-05-03": {"html":"\n<h2>Certificate Transparency & Key Pinning</h2>\n\n<p>The global X.509 ecosystem relies on hundreds of commercial CAs. Under standard PKI semantics, <strong>any universally trusted CA can issue a valid certificate for any domain on Earth</strong>. In 2011, Dutch CA DigiNotar was compromised by threat actors who generated fraudulent certificates for <code>*.google.com</code> and hundreds of high-profile targets, facilitating silent state-level surveillance. To subject every CA issuance to public, verifiable scrutiny, Google formalized <strong>Certificate Transparency (CT)</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive analogy: <strong>The Publicly Audited Ledger of Official Stamps</strong>.\nIn the past, a rogue official could issue a counterfeit property deed in a hidden basement without the rightful owner's knowledge.\n<strong>Certificate Transparency (CT)</strong> establishes a global rule: Any CA issuing an X.509 certificate <strong>must immediately log that certificate into an append-only, publicly verifiable Merkle Tree ledger</strong>!\nThe audit ledger issues a cryptographic <strong>Signed Certificate Timestamp (SCT)</strong>. Since 2018, modern web browsers unconditionally reject any certificate lacking valid SCT proofs. Furthermore, automated bots continuously monitor CT logs on behalf of domain owners, alerting security operations teams within seconds if an unauthorized certificate is minted anywhere globally!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Analyze historic PKI authority failures (DigiNotar, WoSign) and systemic single-point risks<br>\n  ② Master Certificate Transparency architecture (RFC 6962) and append-only Merkle Trees<br>\n  ③ Evaluate SCT delivery mechanisms: X.509 extensions, TLS extensions, and OCSP Stapling<br>\n  ④ Assess public key pinning paradigms: The deprecation of HPKP vs modern mobile app SSL Pinning</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Certificate Defense & Transparency Reference (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Defense Technique</th><th>Deployment Layer</th><th>Core Architectural Principle</th><th>Current Industry Status</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Certificate Transparency (CT)</strong></td>\n      <td>CAs & Public Merkle Tree Logs</td>\n      <td>All minted certificates must be permanently inscribed into verifiable public logs with SCT</td>\n      <td><strong>Mandatory Global Baseline</strong>; required by Chrome and Safari</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>HTTP Public Key Pinning (HPKP)</strong></td>\n      <td>Web Browser (HTTP Header)</td>\n      <td>Instructs browsers to pin specific public key hashes for a domain</td>\n      <td><strong>Deprecated</strong>; prone to catastrophic self-denial of service (pin-ransom)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Mobile SSL Pinning</strong></td>\n      <td>Native iOS / Android App Binary</td>\n      <td>App hardcodes server public key hashes, bypassing OS root truststore</td>\n      <td><strong>Gold Standard for Mobile Banking</strong>; defeats proxy MITM interception</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>DNS CAA Records</strong></td>\n      <td>Authoritative DNS Server</td>\n      <td>Domain owner specifies which specific CAs are authorized to issue certificates</td>\n      <td><strong>Mandatory Enforcement</strong>; verified by compliant CAs prior to issuance</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Passive Asset Discovery via Public CT Logs in Python</h3>\n\n<p>Because every compliant SSL certificate is published to global CT logs, security researchers and penetration testers utilize CT search engines (such as <code>crt.sh</code>) for non-intrusive, zero-packet subdomain discovery:</p>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n  <div class=\"copy-btn\">Copy</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\"># Passive subdomain reconnaissance via Certificate Transparency logs\nimport requests\n\ndef query_ct_subdomains(domain):\n    url = f\"https://crt.sh/?q=%25.{domain}&output=json\"\n    print(f\"[*] Querying Certificate Transparency logs for {domain}...\")\n    try:\n        resp = requests.get(url, timeout=15)\n        if resp.status_code != 200:\n            return []\n        data = resp.json()\n        subdomains = set()\n        for entry in data:\n            for sub in entry.get('name_value', '').split('\\n'):\n                sub = sub.strip()\n                if sub and not sub.startswith('*'):\n                    subdomains.add(sub)\n        return sorted(list(subdomains))\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"[-] Query error: {e}\")\n        return []\n\n# Query sample domain targets\ndiscovered = query_ct_subdomains(\"github.com\")\nprint(f\"[+] Discovered {len(discovered)} unique subdomains registered in CT logs!\")\nfor item in discovered[:5]:\n    print(f\"  - {item}\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercises:</strong><br>\n  During mobile penetration testing, why does installing a custom CA certificate inside an Android device fail to decrypt HTTPS traffic from banking applications, and how do analysts inspect traffic?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> High-security mobile applications enforce <strong>SSL Pinning</strong>: Internal networking clients (e.g., OkHttp) override the default Android TrustManager to validate server certificate public key hashes against hardcoded binary values, ignoring user-installed root certificates. Security engineers employ dynamic instrumentation frameworks like <strong>Frida (running SSL unpinning scripts)</strong> to hook and neutralize verification methods in application memory at runtime, restoring transparency during authorized assessments.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Certificate Transparency converts private CA issuance into publicly auditable, tamper-resistant Merkle trees.<br>\n  ② DNS CAA records restrict CA issuance authorization, while client-side SSL pinning enforces zero-trust validation.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the primary mechanism enforced by Certificate Transparency (CT)?","options":["Mandating that all public CA-issued certificates be published to append-only, publicly auditable Merkle Tree logs","Publishing all private keys to GitHub repositories","Assigning a single global monopoly CA under UN oversight","Prohibiting domain owners from rotating certificates"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Certificate Transparency requires CAs to submit every minted certificate to append-only Merkle tree ledgers, obtaining Signed Certificate Timestamps (SCT) to deter unauthorized issuance."},{"question":"Which defensive configuration prevents mobile banking applications from being intercepted via user-installed root CA certificates?","options":["SSL / TLS Public Key Pinning","Using plain HTTP transport","Passing plaintext credentials in URLs","Activating weak cipher suites"],"answer":0,"explanation":"SSL Pinning instructs mobile applications to validate server public key fingerprints directly against hardcoded binary hashes, ignoring untrusted root certificates."}]},
  "crypto-06-01": {"html":"\n<h2>National Commercial Ciphers: SM2, SM3 & SM4</h2>\n\n<p>Amid escalating geopolitical cyber warfare, architecting an autonomous, sovereign cryptographic foundation is of paramount importance for critical national information infrastructure. The State Cryptography Administration of China formalized the commercial cryptographic algorithm standard family (collectively designated <strong>\"Guomi / SM series\"</strong>), broadly deployed across banking, critical energy sectors, government registries, and smart transportation, with several specifications ratified by ISO/IEC international bodies.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive analogy: <strong>The Sovereign Cryptographic \"Three Musketeers\"</strong>.\nThe international cryptographic landscape relies on three primary pillars: RSA (asymmetric public-key), SHA-256 (hashing digest), and AES (symmetric block cipher).\nThe commercial cipher suite delivers exact sovereign counterparts matching and exceeding those capabilities:\n① <strong>SM4 (Block Cipher)</strong>: Equivalent to AES. Processes 128-bit blocks through 32 iterations of non-linear substitutions with exceptional hardware throughput.\n② <strong>SM3 (Cryptographic Hash)</strong>: Equivalent to SHA-256. Generates a 256-bit digest with superior avalanche stability and resistance against differential cryptanalysis.\n③ <strong>SM2 (Elliptic Curve Cryptography)</strong>: Equivalent to RSA-2048. Powered by a 256-bit prime-field elliptic curve, delivering cryptographic parity to RSA-3072 using a fraction of the computational footprint.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master the functional mapping between commercial ciphers (SM2/3/4) and international baselines (RSA/SHA/AES)<br>\n  ② Analyze the 128-bit key schedule and 32-round unbalanced Feistel architecture of SM4<br>\n  ③ Deconstruct SM2 elliptic curve formatting conventions (C1C3C2 versus C1C2C3 specifications)<br>\n  ④ Implement comprehensive SM4-CBC and SM3 operations in Python using the <code>gmssl</code> package</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Commercial Cipher vs International Standards Mapping (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>International Equivalent</th><th>Mathematical Category</th><th>Parameters (Block / Key Size)</th><th>ISO / IEC Status</th><th>Production Application</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>SM4</strong></td>\n      <td>AES-128 / 3DES</td>\n      <td>Symmetric Block Cipher</td>\n      <td>128-bit block, 128-bit key, 32 rounds</td>\n      <td>ISO/IEC 18033-3</td>\n      <td>WAPI wireless, full-disk database encryption, financial POS</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>SM3</strong></td>\n      <td>SHA-256 / SHA-3</td>\n      <td>Cryptographic Hash</td>\n      <td>Arbitrary input, <strong>256-bit digest</strong>, 64 steps</td>\n      <td>ISO/IEC 10118-3</td>\n      <td>Certificate signing fingerprints, HMAC-SM3 integrity</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>SM2</strong></td>\n      <td>RSA-2048 / ECDSA</td>\n      <td>Elliptic Curve Asymmetric</td>\n      <td>256-bit prime field (Parity with RSA-3072)</td>\n      <td>ISO/IEC 14888-3</td>\n      <td>TLCP SSL/TLS handshakes, electronic identity, key exchange</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>SM9</strong></td>\n      <td>Identity-Based (IBC)</td>\n      <td>Bilinear Pairings Asymmetric</td>\n      <td><strong>Uses email or phone numbers directly as public keys!</strong></td>\n      <td>ISO/IEC 11770-3</td>\n      <td>IoT authentication, zero-certificate secure messaging</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: SM2 Ciphertext Formatting Pitfall (C1C3C2 vs C1C2C3)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Ciphertext Segment</th><th>Length & Meaning</th><th>Legacy Specification (ASN.1 default)</th><th>Modern Standard (GM/T 0009-2012)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>C1</strong></td>\n      <td>64 bytes (Curve ephemeral point (x_1, y_1))</td>\n      <td>Part 1: Header</td>\n      <td>Part 1: Header</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>C2</strong></td>\n      <td>Variable (Length matches plaintext)</td>\n      <td>Part 3: Trailing</td>\n      <td><strong>Part 2: Immediately following C1</strong></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>C3</strong></td>\n      <td>32 bytes (SM3 MAC integrity checksum)</td>\n      <td>Part 2: Interleaved middle</td>\n      <td><strong>Part 3: Appended at end</strong></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td colspan=\"2\"><strong>Engineering Caveat</strong></td>\n      <td colspan=\"2\">Mismatched formatting triggers MAC verification failures. Interoperability requires explicit agreement on serialization order.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Practical SM4-CBC Encryption with Python gmssl</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n  <div class=\"copy-btn\">Copy</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\"># Practical SM4 symmetric encryption in Python via gmssl\nfrom gmssl import sm4, func\nimport binascii\n\n# 1. Prepare 128-bit key and initialization vector\nsecret_key = b\"1234567890abcdef\"\nplain_text = b\"Confidential National Commercial Cipher Data: SM4 Test\"\niv = b\"fedcba0987654321\"\n\n# 2. Encrypt under CBC mode with PKCS7 padding\ncrypt_sm4 = sm4.CryptSM4()\ncrypt_sm4.set_key(secret_key, sm4.SM4_ENCRYPT)\nciphertext = crypt_sm4.crypt_cbc(iv, plain_text)\n\nprint(f\"SM4-CBC Ciphertext Hex: {binascii.hexlify(ciphertext).decode()}\")\n\n# 3. Decrypt and verify exact recovery\ndecrypt_sm4 = sm4.CryptSM4()\ndecrypt_sm4.set_key(secret_key, sm4.SM4_DECRYPT)\ndecrypted = decrypt_sm4.crypt_cbc(iv, ciphertext)\n\nprint(f\"SM4-CBC Decrypted Text: {decrypted.decode('utf-8')}\")\nassert decrypted == plain_text, \"Decrypted data must match original text!\"\nprint(\"✓ Commercial SM4 cipher pipeline validated successfully!\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercises:</strong><br>\n  How do enterprise edge gateways transition existing public-facing HTTPS websites into a dual-certificate architecture supporting both international TLS and domestic TLCP specifications?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> Gateways compile TLS engines equipped with dual-stack support (e.g., BabaSSL / Tongsuo). Two distinct certificate sets are bound concurrently: One standard international RSA/ECDSA certificate (for standard global browsers), alongside a dual SM2 certificate pair (one signing certificate, one encryption certificate) adhering to TLCP protocols. During the Client Hello phase, the gateway inspects offered cipher suites, routing connections seamlessly without degrading user experience.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Commercial ciphers provide sovereign, robust alternatives covering symmetric (SM4), digest (SM3), and asymmetric (SM2) demands.<br>\n  ② Compliance mandates and financial regulations increasingly require native SM2/SM3/SM4 integration.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Which sovereign commercial cryptographic algorithm matches the role of AES block encryption?","options":["SM4","SM3","SM2","SM9"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The commercial cipher suite aligns SM4 as the 128-bit block cipher (analogous to AES), SM3 as the hash digest (SHA-256), and SM2 as the asymmetric elliptic curve (ECC/RSA)."},{"question":"What compatibility serialization issue commonly arises during SM2 cryptographic integration?","options":["Ordering disparities between legacy C1C3C2 and modern C1C2C3 ciphertext formatting specifications","SM2 requiring strictly odd numbers of ciphertext bytes","SM2 requiring Python 2.7 legacy environments","SM2 prohibiting hexadecimal encoding"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Legacy SM2 implementations interleaved MAC component C3 between C1 and C2 (C1C3C2), whereas updated GM/T 0009-2012 specifies C1C2C3. Incompatible ordering results in MAC verification errors."}]},
  "crypto-06-02": {"html":"\n<h2>Zero-Knowledge Proofs & Homomorphic Encryption</h2>\n\n<p>Traditional cryptography secures data in transit and data at rest: Data is either encrypted (inaccessible and uncomputable) or decrypted (computable but exposed). However, in modern cloud-native architectures and confidential computing environments, untrusted remote servers must evaluate operations over confidential datasets. How do we verify statements <strong>without disclosing underlying secrets</strong>? How do we evaluate algorithms <strong>directly over encrypted ciphertext</strong>? <strong>Zero-Knowledge Proofs (ZKP)</strong> and <strong>Homomorphic Encryption (FHE)</strong> solve these challenges.</p>\n\n<p>Consider two intuitive analogies:\n① <strong>Zero-Knowledge Proof: Ali Baba's Secret Cave</strong>.\nA circular cavern splits into pathways A and B, connected at the back by a locked magic door requiring a secret passphrase. Ali Baba claims to know the passphrase but refuses to disclose it to suspicious guards.\nGuards stand outside and shout: \"Ali Baba, emerge from Path B!\" If Ali Baba knows the phrase, he opens the door and exits Path B regardless of which path he initially took. An impostor has only a 50% chance of guessing correctly. Repeating this protocol 20 times reduces the imposter's odds to less than one-in-a-million! Guards achieve <strong>100% mathematical certainty that Ali Baba knows the secret, without ever hearing a single syllable of the passphrase</strong>!\n② <strong>Homomorphic Encryption: The Glovebox Jewelry Workshop</strong>.\nA patron supplies raw gold to a jeweler, fearful that shavings will be stolen. The gold is locked inside a transparent, indestructible glovebox with airtight rubber gloves. The master jeweler shapes the gold entirely inside the box using internal tools. The patron retrieves the locked box and unlocks it at home—the processing succeeded completely, while the raw material remained protected.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand the mathematical foundations of ZKP: Completeness, Soundness, and Zero-Knowledge<br>\n  ② Compare non-interactive zero-knowledge paradigms: zk-SNARKs versus zk-STARKs<br>\n  ③ Map the homomorphic hierarchy: Partially Homomorphic (PHE) through Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE)<br>\n  ④ Execute additive homomorphic calculations over ciphertexts using the Paillier cryptosystem</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Advanced Privacy-Preserving Cryptography Matrix (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Technology</th><th>Core Privacy Capability</th><th>Standard Implementations</th><th>Computational Complexity</th><th>Primary Production Use Case</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Zero-Knowledge Proofs (ZKP)</strong></td>\n      <td>Prove statement validity without revealing witness data</td>\n      <td>zk-SNARKs (Groth16, PLONK), zk-STARKs</td>\n      <td>Prover computation is intensive; verifier evaluates in milliseconds</td>\n      <td>Blockchain rollups (zkSync), private transactions (Zcash), decentralized identity (DID)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Partially Homomorphic (PHE)</strong></td>\n      <td>Supports single algebraic operation (only addition or only multiplication)</td>\n      <td><strong>Paillier (Additive)</strong>, RSA / ElGamal (Multiplicative)</td>\n      <td>Lightweight; comparable to standard public-key operations</td>\n      <td>Federated learning model aggregation, confidential tallying, anonymous surveys</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Fully Homomorphic (FHE)</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>\"The Holy Grail\"</strong>: Evaluates arbitrary addition and multiplication over ciphertext</td>\n      <td>BGV, BFV, CKKS (Vector floating point), TFHE</td>\n      <td>Very high (Inflates evaluation latency by orders of magnitude)</td>\n      <td>Confidential cloud genomics, privacy-preserving AI inferences</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Paillier Additive Homomorphism Mathematics</h3>\n\n<p>The Paillier cryptosystem provides an additive homomorphic relationship: <strong>Multiplying two ciphertexts decrypts to the sum of their plaintexts!</strong></p>\n<p>Formally expressed:\n[\nD(E(m_1) cdot E(m_2) \bmod n^2) = m_1 + m_2 pmod n\n]\nSimilarly, raising a ciphertext (E(m_1)) to a plaintext scalar power (k) decrypts to the product of (m_1) and (k):\n[\nD(E(m_1)^k \bmod n^2) = k cdot m_1 pmod n\n]\nAn untrusted cloud server can aggregate metrics from multiple enterprises without learning individual values.</p>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Additive Homomorphic Computing in Python</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n  <div class=\"copy-btn\">Copy</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\"># Blind aggregation using the Paillier cryptosystem in Python\nfrom phe import paillier\n\n# 1. Generate asymmetric keypair (Private key remains local)\npublic_key, private_key = paillier.generate_paillier_keypair(n_length=1024)\n\n# 2. Local encryption of confidential metrics\nsalary_alice = 15000\nsalary_bob   = 23000\n\nenc_alice = public_key.encrypt(salary_alice)\nenc_bob   = public_key.encrypt(salary_bob)\n\n# 3. Third-party cloud evaluates addition directly over ciphertexts\n# Under Paillier, the overloaded '+' operator executes modular multiplication internally\ncloud_sum = enc_alice + enc_bob\n\n# 4. Decrypt aggregate total locally\ndecrypted_total = private_key.decrypt(cloud_sum)\n\nprint(f\"Decrypted Sum: {decrypted_total}\")\nassert decrypted_total == (salary_alice + salary_bob), \"Homomorphic sum must match exact plaintexts!\"\nprint(\"✓ Homomorphic evaluation verified: Zero data leakage to untrusted host!\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercises:</strong><br>\n  Why are ZK-Rollups architecturally superior to Optimistic Rollups in decentralized blockchain scaling?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> Optimistic Rollups assume valid execution by default, mandating a 7-day fraud-challenge window that delays withdrawals. In contrast, ZK-Rollups bundle thousands of transactions off-chain and submit a compact <strong>Validity Proof (zk-SNARK)</strong> to the Layer-1 contract. Verification executes in milliseconds, confirming state finality immediately without exposing confidential transaction data.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Zero-Knowledge Proofs enable unforgeable assertion verification with zero information disclosure.<br>\n  ② Homomorphic Encryption unlocks verifiable outsourced computing over encrypted states.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What three core mathematical properties must any Zero-Knowledge Proof (ZKP) satisfy?","options":["Completeness, Soundness, and Zero-Knowledge","Symmetry, Irreversibility, and Lossless Compression","Concurrency, Quantum-Immunity, and One-wayness","Collision Resistance, Avalanche, and Provability"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ZKP requires Completeness (an honest prover convinces verifiers), Soundness (a cheating prover cannot falsify proofs), and Zero-Knowledge (verifiers gain no knowledge beyond assertion validity)."},{"question":"What distinctive algebraic property defines the Paillier cryptosystem?","options":["Multiplying two ciphertexts decrypts directly to the sum of their corresponding plaintexts (Additive Homomorphism)","Ciphertexts can be viewed directly as plaintext strings","Encryption does not require public keys","Decryption operates 1000x faster than AES"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Paillier provides additive homomorphism: D(E(m1) * E(m2) mod n^2) = m1 + m2 mod n. Untrusted cloud nodes can compute sum aggregations over encrypted records."}]},
  "crypto-06-03": {"html":"\n<h2>Post-Quantum Cryptography & Lattice Ciphers</h2>\n\n<p>Since the 1970s, the security of global digital infrastructure has rested upon two foundational number-theoretic problems: <strong>Integer Prime Factorization (RSA)</strong> and the <strong>Discrete Logarithm Problem (DH / ECC)</strong>. However, Peter Shor's 1994 publication of <strong>Shor's Algorithm</strong> revealed that sufficiently powerful quantum computers evaluate quantum Fourier transforms to factor integers and extract discrete logarithms in polynomial time, completely dismantling RSA and ECC. <strong>Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC)</strong> rebuilds cryptographic defense perimeters against quantum capabilities.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive analogy: <strong>Migrating from 1D Walls into Multi-Dimensional Lattice Mazes</strong>.\nClassic RSA functions like a thick stone wall constructed on a 1D number line: Extremely difficult for classical computers to batter down, but quantum superpositions tunnel through it instantly.\nPost-quantum cryptographers retreat into <strong>Lattice-based Cryptography</strong>: Hiding secrets within a geometric grid spanning hundreds of dimensional axes (an infinite multi-dimensional crystal lattice). Finding the closest lattice point (Shortest Vector Problem, SVP) lacks the periodic structures exploited by quantum Fourier transforms, leaving quantum algorithms stranded.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Analyze the dual quantum threats: Shor's algorithm (breaking asymmetric PKI) and Grover's algorithm (halving symmetric keys)<br>\n  ② Assess the existential threat of \"Harvest Now, Decrypt Later\" (HNDL) reconnaissance<br>\n  ③ Navigate the four standardized NIST PQC algorithms: Kyber, Dilithium, Falcon, and SPHINCS+<br>\n  ④ Evaluate engineering tradeoffs between classical key sizes and post-quantum packet overheads</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Quantum Threat Spectrum & Mitigation Reference (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Cryptographic Algorithm</th><th>Quantum Threat</th><th>Classical Security</th><th>Quantum Security</th><th>Post-Quantum Migration Path</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>RSA-2048 / 4096</strong></td>\n      <td>Shor's Algorithm (Polynomial Time)</td>\n      <td>112 / 128 bits</td>\n      <td><strong>0 bits (Completely Broken)</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>ML-KEM (CRYSTALS-Kyber)</strong> Key Encapsulation</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>ECDSA / Ed25519</strong></td>\n      <td>Shor's Algorithm (Polynomial Time)</td>\n      <td>128 bits</td>\n      <td><strong>0 bits (Completely Broken)</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>ML-DSA (CRYSTALS-Dilithium)</strong> / Falcon</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>AES-128 / 256</strong></td>\n      <td>Grover's Algorithm (Square Root Speedup)</td>\n      <td>128 / 256 bits</td>\n      <td><strong>64 / 128 bits</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>Standardize on AES-256</strong> (128-bit quantum security is impenetrable)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>SHA-256 / SHA-3</strong></td>\n      <td>Grover's Algorithm (Square Root Speedup)</td>\n      <td>256 bits</td>\n      <td>128-bit pre-image resistance</td>\n      <td>Retain SHA-256 or upgrade to SHA-384 / SHA-512</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: The Four NIST PQC Standardized Algorithms</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Standard Designation</th><th>Underlying Mathematics</th><th>Operational Role</th><th>Performance & Profile</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>ML-KEM<br>(CRYSTALS-Kyber)</strong></td>\n      <td>Module Learning with Errors (M-LWE)</td>\n      <td>Key Encapsulation Mechanism (KEM)</td>\n      <td><strong>Primary standard for TLS</strong>; 800-byte public key; rapid key generation</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>ML-DSA<br>(CRYSTALS-Dilithium)</strong></td>\n      <td>Module Learning with Errors (M-LWE)</td>\n      <td>General Digital Signature</td>\n      <td><strong>Primary standard for signatures</strong>; robust performance; 2420-byte signature</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>FN-DSA<br>(Falcon)</strong></td>\n      <td>Shortest Vector Problem over NTRU lattices</td>\n      <td>Compact Digital Signature</td>\n      <td>Smallest signature footprint (~666 bytes); requires complex floating-point hardware</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>SLH-DSA<br>(SPHINCS+)</strong></td>\n      <td>Stateless Hash-based Merkle Trees</td>\n      <td>Fail-Safe Backup Signature</td>\n      <td>Relies exclusively on hash security assumptions; substantial signature overhead</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Threat Strategy: \"Harvest Now, Decrypt Later\" (HNDL)</h3>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Strategic Alert: Harvest Now, Decrypt Later</div>\n  <p>Many organizations assume that because production-scale quantum computers are a decade away, mitigation can wait. This represents a catastrophic strategic miscalculation. Advanced state-sponsored threat actors actively execute <strong>Harvest Now, Decrypt Later (HNDL)</strong> campaigns: Intercepting and warehousing encrypted diplomatic, financial, and intelligence transmissions today. Once a cryptanalytically relevant quantum computer becomes operational, archived traffic will be decrypted retrospectively. Data requiring confidentiality horizons of 10+ years is compromised today unless secured with hybrid post-quantum encapsulation.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 4: Public Key Size Overhead Comparison in Python</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n  <div class=\"copy-btn\">Copy</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\"># Parameter size comparison: Classical vs Post-Quantum KEMs\necc_pubkey_bytes   = 32     # X25519\nrsa2048_pubkey_bytes = 256  # RSA-2048\nkyber768_pubkey_bytes = 1184 # Kyber-768 (Level 3 Post-Quantum Standard)\n\nprint(f\"Classical X25519:   {ecc_pubkey_bytes} bytes\")\nprint(f\"Classical RSA-2048: {rsa2048_pubkey_bytes} bytes\")\nprint(f\"PQC Kyber-768:      {kyber768_pubkey_bytes} bytes (Over 37x larger than ECC!)\")\nprint(\"\\nEngineering Takeaway: Upgrading to PQC necessitates re-evaluating network MTUs and packet fragmentation.\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercises:</strong><br>\n  Why have leading browser vendors and CDNs deployed <strong>Hybrid Key Encapsulation (e.g., X25519Kyber768)</strong> instead of switching exclusively to pure post-quantum algorithms?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> Post-quantum lattice constructions are relatively novel and have not withstood decades of adversarial cryptanalysis. A <strong>Hybrid Construction</strong> computes two independent shared secrets (one via classical X25519, one via Kyber768) and combines them through a key derivation function. An adversary must compromise <strong>both</strong> the classical discrete logarithm problem AND the lattice-learning problem to break the session. If unexpected algebraic vulnerabilities emerge in Kyber, classical cryptography maintains security; if quantum hardware arrives, Kyber protects the channel.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Shor's algorithm renders classical RSA and ECC obsolete against quantum computing.<br>\n  ② Hybrid Key Encapsulation represents the immediate best practice for safeguarding against retrospective HNDL exploitation.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Against which category of classical cryptosystems does Peter Shor's quantum algorithm pose an existential threat?","options":["Asymmetric cryptosystems relying on integer factorization or discrete logarithms (RSA, ECC, and DH)","AES-256 block encryption","SHA-3 hash algorithms","One-Time Pads (OTP)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Shor's algorithm evaluates quantum Fourier transforms to factor integers and compute discrete logarithms in polynomial time, completely compromising classical RSA and ECC/DH."},{"question":"Which lattice-based algorithm was selected by NIST as the primary general standard for post-quantum Key Encapsulation Mechanisms (KEM)?","options":["ML-KEM (CRYSTALS-Kyber)","DES","RSA-4096","MD5"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NIST standardized CRYSTALS-Kyber under the designation ML-KEM, leveraging Module Learning with Errors (M-LWE) lattice problems as the primary post-quantum key exchange standard."}]}
});
